window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2021"] = {"id": "jammu-2021", "title": "University of Jammu 2021", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu 2021, M.A. Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social, Health and Organisational Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics", "aptitude": "General Awareness"}, "questions": [{"q": "The statement \"Psychology is the positive science of behaviour\" is attributed to:", "options": {"a": "Skinner", "b": "Watson", "c": "Wundt", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the definition of psychology as \"the positive science of behaviour\" is attributed to John B. Watson (1913). In his paper \"Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It\" (1913), Watson launched behaviourism by throwing out consciousness and introspection. He called psychology \"a purely objective experimental branch of natural science\", whose subject matter is behaviour and whose theoretical goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. \"Positive\" carries the sense made popular by Auguste Comte (1830 to 1842): a positive science describes what is, from observed facts, while a normative science (ethics, logic, aesthetics) says what ought to be. For example, Riya, who counts how often a child hits classmates instead of asking him to describe his inner feelings, is working in Watson's positive science. Compare the other classic definitions. Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the Leipzig laboratory, studied conscious experience by trained introspection. B. F. Skinner (1938) built radical behaviourism and operant conditioning on Watson's base; Indian textbooks quote him as calling psychology the science of behaviour and experience. Option b is right because Watson is the name textbooks attach to the shift from mind to behaviour, studied as a natural, positive science. Exam trap: Skinner is the most famous behaviourist, so students pick him, but the founding definition belongs to Watson; and Wundt's subject was consciousness, the very thing Watson rejected. Key note: the \"positive science\" phrase itself appears in William McDougall's An Introduction to Social Psychology (1908), which calls psychology \"the positive science of conduct or behaviour\" (a definition he first proposed in 1905), whereas Watson's 1913 paper says \"a purely objective experimental branch of natural science\". McDougall is not offered, so d is arguable, but Indian textbooks attach this line to Watson, and b stands.", "topic": "Definitions of psychology", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "Mathematical Psychology borrows its methods from:", "options": {"a": "Mathematics", "b": "Operations Research", "c": "Information Technology", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: mathematical psychology borrows its methods from mathematics, operations research and information science, so all of these. Mathematical psychology builds formal, testable models of perception, learning, memory, choice and decision; its own journal, the Journal of Mathematical Psychology, began in 1964. Its roots lie in Gustav Fechner's (1860) law that sensation grows with the logarithm of stimulus intensity. Mathematics supplies probability, algebra and calculus: William Estes's (1950) stimulus sampling theory of learning and Duncan Luce's (1959) choice axiom are pure probability models. Operations research, the wartime science of optimising decisions, supplied optimisation and queueing models, alongside the game and decision theory of John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern (1944). Signal detection theory came from radar engineering; Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) brought it into psychology, and David Green and John Swets (1966) wrote its classic text, which separated an observer's sensitivity from their response bias. Information theory (Claude Shannon, 1948) let George Miller (1956) measure the capacity of absolute judgement in bits, and computer science added simulation and computational modelling. For example, when Meera models a radiologist's yes or no calls on X-rays, she uses probability (mathematics), cost-weighted decision rules (operations research) and computer model fitting (information technology) together. Option d is right because each of the three fields has lent methods to the discipline, so no single option is complete. Exam trap: the name suggests mathematics alone, so a looks safe, but the field openly borrows from engineering and computing as well. Key note: \"information technology\" is loose wording for information theory and computing, but each listed field has lent methods, so d stands.", "topic": "Mathematical psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Stressing the use of a product in relation to the cognitive capacity & ability of users is a domain of:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Psychology", "b": "Cognitive Ergonomics", "c": "Cognitive Biometrics", "d": "Quantum Cognition"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: designing a product around the cognitive capacities and abilities of its users is the domain of cognitive ergonomics. Ergonomics, also called human factors, fits tools, work and environments to people; the International Ergonomics Association divides it into physical, cognitive and organisational ergonomics. Cognitive ergonomics deals with perception, attention, memory, mental workload and decision making while people use a system. It asks whether a display can be read at a glance, whether a menu overloads working memory, and whether a control panel forgives errors made under stress, as in cockpit displays, mobile app menus and medical devices. Its classic text is Donald Norman's (1988) book The Design of Everyday Things (first published as The Psychology of Everyday Things), which showed that doors, stoves and switches fail when their design ignores how people think. For example, Kabir redesigns a hospital drug-pump screen so that a tired nurse cannot confuse 10 mg with 100 mg, and groups the buttons so the next step is obvious: that is cognitive ergonomics. Option b is right because the stem is about a product's use matched to users' mental capacity, which is the applied, design side of cognition. Exam trap: cognitive psychology studies the same mental processes, so a looks correct, but it studies them in general, not as a discipline for designing products and interfaces.", "topic": "Cognitive ergonomics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following experiments was carried out by Jane Elliot (1968) in order to demonstrate the effects of racism & prejudice?", "options": {"a": "A Class Divided", "b": "False Consensus Experiment", "c": "A Class Compared", "d": "Invisible Gorilla Experiment"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Jane Elliott's 1968 classroom exercise on racism and prejudice is known as \"A Class Divided\". Jane Elliott (1968) was a third-grade teacher in Riceville, Iowa. On 5 April 1968, the day after Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated, she told her all-white class that people of one eye colour were better than the rest. Children with the favoured eye colour got extra privileges, while the others wore collars and were criticised; the next day she reversed the roles. Within hours the \"superior\" children became bossy and cruel to their friends, and the \"inferior\" children grew timid and did worse on classroom tasks. The exercise showed how an arbitrary, visible category can produce prejudice and discrimination, and what it feels like to be on the receiving end. It reached a wide audience through the ABC documentary The Eye of the Storm (1970) and the PBS Frontline documentary A Class Divided (1985), the title by which it is now known. Its lesson matches the minimal group paradigm of Henri Tajfel and colleagues (1971): categorisation alone is enough to breed in-group favouritism. Option a is right because \"A Class Divided\" is the name under which Elliott's blue eyes, brown eyes exercise is taught. Exam trap: \"A Class Compared\" is a made-up look-alike title; only \"Divided\" is real, and the other two options are famous studies on different topics.", "topic": "Jane Elliott's eye colour exercise", "fig": "Jane Elliott"}, {"q": "MLP stands for:", "options": {"a": "Multilayer Perceptron", "b": "Microlayer Perceptron", "c": "Multilayer Perception", "d": "Movement Related Perception"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: MLP stands for multilayer perceptron. A multilayer perceptron is a feedforward artificial neural network with an input layer, one or more hidden layers and an output layer. Each unit adds up its weighted inputs, passes the sum through a non-linear activation function and sends the result forward to the next layer. The network learns by backpropagation of error, popularised by David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams (1986): the error at the output is passed back layer by layer, and every connection weight is nudged to reduce it. The single unit is the perceptron of Frank Rosenblatt (1958). Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert (1969) proved that a single-layer perceptron cannot solve problems that are not linearly separable, such as exclusive-or (XOR); adding hidden layers removes that limit. MLPs are central to connectionism, or parallel distributed processing (David Rumelhart and James McClelland, 1986), which models knowledge as connection weights spread across many simple units working at once. For example, Meera trains an MLP to read handwritten digits: 784 pixel inputs, a hidden layer of 100 units and 10 output units, one for each digit. Option a is right because \"multilayer\" names the stacked layers and \"perceptron\" names the unit they are built from. Exam trap: option c swaps perceptron (the artificial neuron) for perception (the psychological process of interpreting sensations); one changed syllable changes the meaning.", "topic": "Multilayer perceptron", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a formula for the learning curve?", "options": {"a": "Y = a + bX", "b": "Y = aXᵇ", "c": "Y = a + bX + c", "d": "Y = aX + t"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the learning curve is described by the power function Y = aX^b. Here Y is the time (or number of errors) on trial X, a is the time on the first trial, and b is a negative exponent that sets how fast learning proceeds. Because a power curve falls steeply at first and then flattens, it captures the classic negatively accelerated learning curve: big gains early, smaller and smaller gains later, but improvement that never quite stops. Allen Newell and Paul Rosenbloom (1981) called this the power law of practice; plotted on log-log axes it becomes a straight line. In industry the same formula is the aircraft learning curve of Theodore Wright (1936): every doubling of output cuts the average time per unit by a fixed percentage. For example, if Kabir needs 60 seconds to type a passage on trial 1 and b is minus 0.5, he needs 30 seconds on trial 4 and 15 seconds on trial 16: every fourfold increase in practice halves his time. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) was the first to describe a learning curve, from his own memorising of nonsense syllables. Option b is right because only the power form gives fast early improvement that slows with practice. Exam trap: option a looks familiar from regression, but Y = a + bX is a straight line, meaning the same gain on every trial, which real learning does not show.", "topic": "Learning curve formula", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In context of linkage of motivation with brain structures, choose the correct sequence:", "options": {"a": "Cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loop", "b": "Cortico-thalamo-basal ganglia-cortical loop", "c": "Cortico-thalamo loop", "d": "Cortico-thalamo-cortical loop"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: motivation is linked to brain structures through the cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loop. Garrett Alexander, Mahlon DeLong and Peter Strick (1986) described several parallel loops of this shape: motor, oculomotor, dorsolateral prefrontal, lateral orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate (limbic). In each, the cortex sends signals to the striatum (caudate, putamen and, in its ventral part, the nucleus accumbens); the striatum projects to the globus pallidus and substantia nigra; these output nuclei project to the thalamus; and the thalamus sends the signal back to the same cortical area. The loop works like a gate, letting one plan or action through while holding rivals back. The limbic loop, running through the nucleus accumbens and ventral pallidum, receives dopamine from the ventral tegmental area and turns reward signals into motivated action, the \"wanting\" that gets behaviour going. For example, when Arjun hears the ping of a game notification, dopamine in his ventral striatum tags it as rewarding, and the loop pushes the plan \"pick up the phone\" through to action. Option a is right because it gives the true order: cortex, then basal ganglia, then thalamus, then back to cortex. Exam trap: option b uses the same parts in the wrong order, placing the thalamus before the basal ganglia; in the loop the thalamus is the last relay before the cortex, not the first stop.", "topic": "Basal ganglia motivation loop", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which method is used to identify the areas of brain related to intelligence?", "options": {"a": "Electrical Stimulation", "b": "Voxel-based morphometry", "c": "Cryogenic Blockade", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: voxel-based morphometry is a method used to identify brain areas related to intelligence. A voxel is a tiny three-dimensional pixel of a brain scan. Voxel-based morphometry (VBM), set out by John Ashburner and Karl Friston (2000), takes structural MRI scans from many people, fits them to a common template brain, and compares the amount of grey matter at every voxel. If grey matter at a spot rises or falls with a test score, that spot is linked to the trait being measured. Richard Haier and colleagues (2004) used VBM to correlate grey matter with IQ and found the related regions spread across the frontal, temporal, parietal and occipital lobes, rather than in a single \"intelligence centre\". This work fed the parieto-frontal integration theory (P-FIT) of Rex Jung and Richard Haier (2007), which says intelligence depends on a network linking the parietal and frontal lobes. For example, Kabir's lab scans 100 students, tests their IQ, and runs VBM to see which voxels hold more grey matter in the higher scorers. Option b is right because VBM is a non-invasive, whole-brain imaging method that can relate brain structure to intelligence scores in healthy people. Exam trap: electrical stimulation sounds like brain mapping, and it is, but it maps movement, sensation and speech at single points, not general intelligence.", "topic": "Voxel-based morphometry and intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a personality trait?", "options": {"a": "Extraversion", "b": "Agreeableness", "c": "Neuroticism", "d": "Optimism"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: optimism is not one of the basic personality traits listed here. The other three options belong to the Big Five, or five-factor model, the most widely accepted map of personality traits. Researchers analysing trait words in the language kept finding five broad dimensions; Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) measured them with the NEO Personality Inventory. They are openness to experience, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism (OCEAN). Each is a continuous dimension: extraversion runs from sociable and energetic to quiet and reserved, agreeableness from warm and cooperative to cold and hostile, and neuroticism from anxious and moody to calm and stable. Optimism is different. Dispositional optimism, described by Michael Scheier and Charles Carver (1985) and measured with the Life Orientation Test, is a generalised expectancy that good things will happen. It is a narrower belief about outcomes, studied mainly in health psychology, not one of the broad basic traits. For example, Arjun may be an introvert (low on extraversion) and still be confident that his exam will go well. Option d is right because optimism is the only option that is not a Big Five dimension. Exam trap: neuroticism sounds like a disorder, so students pick c, but it is a normal trait dimension of emotional instability. Key note: optimism is sometimes loosely called a trait, but it is not one of the Big Five, which is what the other three options are; d stands.", "topic": "Big Five personality traits", "fig": "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae"}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about superego?", "options": {"a": "It regulates ideals", "b": "It regulates morals", "c": "Its demands oppose the id", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements hold true of the superego. In the structural model of Sigmund Freud (The Ego and the Id, 1923), personality has three parts. The id is the inborn reservoir of drives and works on the pleasure principle; the ego works on the reality principle and mediates; the superego is the moral arm and works on the morality principle. The superego forms at about age 5 to 6, when the Oedipus complex is resolved and the child internalises parents' and society's standards, so that parental control becomes self-control. It has two parts. The ego ideal holds the standards of perfection the child was praised for and rewards us with pride when we live up to them: it regulates ideals (a). The conscience holds the behaviours the child was punished for and punishes us with guilt: it regulates morals (b). Like the id it allows no compromise, and its demands run directly against the id's selfish, pleasure-seeking impulses (c). For example, when Kabir finds a wallet full of cash, his id says keep it, his superego says returning it is right and keeping it would bring guilt, and his ego works out a practical way to return it. Option d is right because ideals, morals and opposition to the id are all functions of the superego. Exam trap: the ego, not the superego, is the mediator; the superego is as unbending as the id, only in the opposite direction.", "topic": "Freud's superego", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Social behaviour encompasses:", "options": {"a": "Pro-social behaviour", "b": "Anti-social behaviour", "c": "Emotional behaviour", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: social behaviour encompasses prosocial, antisocial and emotional behaviour, so all of these. Social behaviour is any behaviour directed at other people or shaped by their actual, imagined or implied presence, the subject matter of social psychology in the classic definition of Gordon Allport (1954). Prosocial behaviour is any act meant to benefit others: helping, sharing, comforting, volunteering. John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) studied when bystanders do and do not help in an emergency. Antisocial behaviour harms others or breaks social rules: aggression, bullying, cheating, vandalism. It is still social, because it is aimed at other people. Emotional behaviour counts too when it is expressed towards or because of others: comforting a crying friend, anger at an insult, shared joy, and catching the mood of a crowd (emotional contagion). Even the way emotions are shown follows social display rules (Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen, 1969). For example, at a family wedding Meera helps her aunt serve food (prosocial), a cousin shoves his way to the stage (antisocial), and everyone weeps at the bride's farewell (emotional): all three are social behaviour. Option d is right because each option is one kind of behaviour directed at or influenced by others. Exam trap: students often treat social behaviour as only the friendly or helpful kind, but harmful and emotional acts in social settings are social behaviour too.", "topic": "Scope of social behaviour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The person perception is associated with:", "options": {"a": "Categorization of the Behaviour of People", "b": "Inference formation about qualities of people", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Cognitive processes only"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: person perception is associated with both categorising people's behaviour and forming inferences about their qualities. Person perception is the set of processes by which we form impressions of other people: their traits, intentions and feelings. Yaacov Trope (1986) described two stages: first we identify or categorise the behaviour (\"she is helping\"), then we infer a disposition from it (\"she is kind\"). Daniel Gilbert and colleagues (1988) added a third step: we correct that inference for the situation, but only when we have the time and mental energy to do so. Solomon Asch (1946) showed how one inference spreads: a stranger described as \"warm\" was also judged generous and happy, while \"cold\" turned the whole impression around. Fritz Heider (1958) pictured the perceiver as a naive scientist hunting for causes. For example, when Kabir sees a new classmate lend his notes to everyone, he categorises the act as generous and infers that the classmate is a kind person. Option c is right because categorisation and inference are the two linked steps of every impression, so neither a nor b alone is complete. Exam trap: option d sounds scientific, but person perception is not purely cognitive; the perceiver's mood, motives and goals shape it too, as when a good mood makes everyone seem nicer.", "topic": "Person perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choose the odd one out of the following:", "options": {"a": "Heider", "b": "Jones", "c": "Davis", "d": "Watson"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: John B. Watson (1913) is the odd one out, because Fritz Heider (1958), Edward E. Jones and Keith Davis (1965) are attribution theorists, while Watson founded behaviourism. Attribution is the process by which people infer the causes of their own and others' behaviour. Heider, in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), founded attribution theory: he saw ordinary people as naive scientists and split causes into dispositional (inside the person) and situational (outside the person). Jones and Davis (1965) built on this with correspondent inference theory: we infer that an act reveals a matching trait when it is freely chosen, has non-common effects and is low in social desirability. Jones later showed the correspondence bias with Victor Harris (1967) in the Castro essay study. Watson, by contrast, launched behaviourism in 1913 and banished inner mental causes from psychology, the very thing attribution theory studies. For example, if Riya decides that a classmate failed because he is lazy rather than because the paper was hard, she is making the kind of dispositional attribution Heider described. Option d is right because Watson is the only name not linked to attribution theory. Exam trap: the Davis here is the social psychologist Keith Davis, not the management writer Keith Davis (1953) of grapevine research; and the attribution name students expect most, Harold Kelley (1967), is simply absent, so do not hunt for him.", "topic": "Attribution theorists", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "The statement \"Interview board may give less marks to a particular group of people\" illustrates:", "options": {"a": "Bias", "b": "Stereotype", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Cognitive dissonance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: an interview board giving lower marks to a particular group of people illustrates bias. A bias is a systematic tilt in judgement or treatment for or against a person or group, regardless of actual merit. Social psychologists split group bias into three parts that match the ABC of attitudes. A stereotype is the cognitive part: a belief about the traits typical of a group's members (\"group X is less capable\"), what Walter Lippmann (1922) called \"pictures in our heads\". Prejudice is the affective part: a negative attitude towards people because of their group (Gordon Allport, 1954). Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair action against them. The statement in the stem describes an action: the board marks one group lower whatever the interviewees actually say. That is biased, discriminatory treatment, the outward face of bias. Option a is right because the statement describes unfair, systematic treatment of a group, which is what bias means, and nothing in it states a belief about the group's traits. Exam trap: students see \"particular group\" and jump to stereotype, but a stereotype is a belief in the head, whereas lower marks are a behaviour. Key note: such biased marking often rests on a stereotype, so c is arguable, but the statement describes unfair treatment, not a belief about the group's traits; a stands.", "topic": "Bias versus stereotype", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Interpersonal Processes:", "options": {"a": "Involve social reinforcement", "b": "Don't involve social reinforcement", "c": "Are not a domain of social psychology", "d": "Are devoid of theoretical explanations"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: interpersonal processes involve social reinforcement. Interpersonal processes are what goes on between people: attraction and liking, friendship and love, communication, helping, conflict and influence. Much of this runs on social reinforcement, the rewards people give each other: approval, praise, attention, a smile, company. In the reinforcement-affect model of Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore (1970), we like people who make us feel good, and even people who are simply present when something pleasant happens. Social exchange theory (John Thibaut and Harold Kelley, 1959; George Homans, 1961) treats relationships as trades of rewards and costs: we stay where the rewards outweigh the costs compared with our alternatives. For example, Riya keeps sitting with Arjun in the canteen because he laughs at her jokes and praises her drawings; his attention reinforces her approach, and her company rewards him in turn. Interpersonal processes are a core domain of social psychology, with whole chapters on attraction, close relationships, helping and aggression, and they are explained by many theories, from exchange and equity to attachment. Option a is right because social reinforcement is one of the main forces that starts relationships and keeps them going. Exam trap: options c and d are sweeping absolute claims; the topic is central to social psychology and rich in theory, so both are false.", "topic": "Interpersonal processes and reinforcement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following genes is associated with aggressive behaviour?", "options": {"a": "SHANK3 gene", "b": "MAOA gene", "c": "SHANK2 gene", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MAOA gene is the one associated with aggressive behaviour. MAOA, on the X chromosome, codes the enzyme monoamine oxidase A, which breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine after they are released. Low enzyme activity leaves these transmitters poorly cleared and is linked to impulsive aggression. Han Brunner and colleagues (1993) studied a Dutch family whose men carried a mutation that abolished the enzyme; they showed borderline intellectual disability and impulsive aggressive outbursts, a pattern now called Brunner syndrome. Avshalom Caspi, Terrie Moffitt and colleagues (2002), in the Dunedin study in New Zealand, found that boys with the low-activity MAOA variant who were maltreated in childhood were far more likely to become antisocial adults; without maltreatment the variant carried little extra risk. This is a gene-environment interaction, not a simple gene for violence. Because males have only one X chromosome, a single low-activity copy shows its effect, so most studies are of men and boys. For example, Kabir and Arjun both carry the low-activity variant, but only Kabir, who was beaten as a child, grows into a seriously aggressive adult. Option b is right because MAOA is the gene repeatedly tied to aggression, popularly nicknamed the \"warrior gene\". Exam trap: SHANK genes also turn up in behaviour genetics, but they are linked to autism, not aggression.", "topic": "MAOA gene and aggression", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not the stage of group formation?", "options": {"a": "Forming", "b": "Norming", "c": "Storming", "d": "Alarming"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: alarming is not a stage of group formation. Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed studies of therapy, training and laboratory groups and proposed that groups develop through four stages. In forming, members meet, are polite and unsure, and look to the leader for direction. In storming, conflict surfaces as members compete over roles, goals and influence. In norming, the group settles its conflicts, agrees on rules and roles, and becomes cohesive. In performing, the group works smoothly and puts its energy into the task. With Mary Ann Jensen in 1977, Tuckman added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the group finishes its task and disbands. For example, Meera's college project team is polite at the first meeting (forming), argues over who will present (storming), agrees on a timetable and roles (norming), and then finishes the work efficiently (performing); after submission it breaks up (adjourning). Option d is right because alarming is not in Tuckman's sequence; it rhymes with the real stages but is a fake. Exam trap: \"alarm\" is a real stage name elsewhere, the alarm reaction of the general adaptation syndrome described by Hans Selye (1936), so it feels familiar; do not carry stress stages into group development.", "topic": "Tuckman's stages of group development", "fig": "Bruce Tuckman"}, {"q": "The statement \"A father loves his son, but sometimes dislikes him for not being serious about studies\" exemplifies:", "options": {"a": "Approach-approach conflict", "b": "Approach-avoidance conflict", "c": "Avoidance-avoidance conflict", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the father's feelings show an approach-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) classified conflicts by whether goals pull us towards them (approach) or push us away (avoidance). In an approach-avoidance conflict a single goal or person has both attractive and unattractive features, so it draws us and repels us at the same time; the result is ambivalence and wavering. Here the son is one object: the father loves him (approach) yet dislikes his casual attitude to studies (avoidance). Neal Miller (1944) explained the wavering with gradients: both tendencies grow stronger as the goal comes closer, but avoidance grows faster, so the person hovers where the two cross. The other types differ in the number and value of the goals. Approach-approach is a choice between two attractive goals and is the easiest to resolve. Avoidance-avoidance is a choice between two unwanted goals, the \"devil and the deep sea\", and leads to delay or attempts to escape. Double approach-avoidance involves two options, each with good and bad points, and is the commonest in real life. Option b is right because the stem describes one person who evokes both love and dislike, mixed feelings towards a single object. Exam trap: the word \"loves\" tempts approach-approach, but that type needs two attractive alternatives, and here there is only one object with good and bad sides.", "topic": "Lewin's types of conflict", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "The informal communication network in an organization is called:", "options": {"a": "Grapevine", "b": "Rumour", "c": "Centralized communication", "d": "Wheel communication"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the informal communication network in an organisation is called the grapevine. Every organisation has a formal network, the official chain of command along which circulars, orders and reports travel. Beside it runs an informal network built on friendships, lunch groups and chance meetings; information on it ignores rank and spreads fast. The name is usually traced to the American Civil War, when makeshift telegraph wires hung loosely from trees like grapevines. The management scholar Keith Davis (1953) studied it and found it fast and, on routine matters, mostly accurate. He described four chains: single strand (A tells B, who tells C), gossip (one person tells everyone), probability (people pass news on at random) and cluster (each person tells a few chosen others), and found the cluster chain the most common. For example, Arjun hears in the office canteen that bonuses are coming a week before the official circular appears: the news reached him on the grapevine. Option a is right because grapevine is the standard name for the informal network itself. Exam trap: rumour is the near-miss; a rumour is an unverified message that may travel on the grapevine, but the network is not the same as the message. Note also that this Keith Davis is not the social psychologist Keith Davis of correspondent inference theory.", "topic": "Grapevine communication", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A leader is anyone who:", "options": {"a": "Identifies the problem", "b": "Gives the solution", "c": "Identifies the problem & gives the solution", "d": "Necessarily has high IQ"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: a leader is anyone who identifies the problem and gives the solution. Leadership is the process of influencing a group towards a shared goal. A working definition used in management texts says a leader is the person who identifies the problem the group faces and guides the group to a solution. Both halves are needed. Spotting a problem without offering a way forward is only criticism or diagnosis; handing out answers to problems someone else has defined is expert advice, not leadership. For example, when Kabir notices that his cricket team keeps losing in the last five overs and then organises extra death-bowling practice, he is leading: he named the problem and steered the team to a fix. Ralph Stogdill (1948), reviewing trait research, found leaders only slightly above their group in intelligence; traits predicted leadership weakly, and a leader in one situation might not lead in another. No fixed IQ is required, and a very large gap from followers can even reduce a leader's influence. Option c is right because it is the only option that joins recognising the group's problem with guiding it to a solution. Exam trap: option d uses \"necessarily\", an absolute word that trait research contradicts. Key note: leadership is broader than problem solving, as in the influence-based definitions of Ralph Stogdill and Bernard Bass (1985), but among these options the fullest description is c; c stands.", "topic": "Definition of a leader", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Activity anorexia is to ____ as Anorexia nervosa is to ____.", "options": {"a": "Rats, Humans", "b": "Cats, Humans", "c": "Ants, Humans", "d": "Dogs, Humans"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: activity anorexia is studied in rats, just as anorexia nervosa is diagnosed in humans. The stem pair links a condition to the species in which it is found or modelled. Activity-based anorexia was first reported by Alvin Routtenberg and Andrew Kuznesof (1967): rats allowed to eat for only a short window each day and given free access to a running wheel ran more and more, ate less and less, lost weight fast and could starve to death, even though food was offered daily. Rats on the same feeding schedule without a wheel adapted and survived. W. Frank Epling, W. David Pierce and Larry Stefan (1983) set out a theory of activity-based anorexia and proposed it as a model of an activity-driven form of human anorexia; Epling and Pierce later popularised the shorter name activity anorexia. The link is that many patients with anorexia nervosa also exercise excessively and become more restless as their weight falls. Anorexia nervosa is the human eating disorder: in DSM-5 (2013), restriction of intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a disturbed experience of body shape or weight. Example: Meera, 17, diets and runs 15 km a day while her weight keeps dropping; her mix of food restriction and compulsive exercise is what the rat wheel model copies in the laboratory. Option a is right because the activity anorexia paradigm is a rat model, and anorexia nervosa is its human counterpart. Exam trap: every option keeps 'Humans' fixed, so the item turns entirely on the laboratory animal; the classic wheel-running model uses rats, not cats, ants or dogs.", "topic": "Activity anorexia animal model", "fig": ""}, {"q": "DSM-V is:", "options": {"a": "Based on research", "b": "Most preferred in Asia", "c": "Contradictory to ICD-10", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: DSM-5 is based on research. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, fifth edition, was published by the American Psychiatric Association in 2013 after more than a decade of preparation: research planning conferences, reviews of the literature, expert work groups for each family of disorders, and field trials that tested how reliably clinicians could use the new criteria. Its stated aim was a more evidence-based manual, and changes such as merging autistic disorder and Asperger's disorder into one autism spectrum disorder were justified by research reviews. Option a is right because DSM-5 was built from research evidence and field testing. The other two statements are false. In most of Asia, India included, the official system in hospitals and health records is the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10, and now ICD-11, in effect from 2022); DSM dominates in the United States and in research journals. Nor is DSM-5 contradictory to ICD: it was deliberately harmonised with it and lists ICD codes (ICD-9-CM and ICD-10-CM) for its diagnoses. Example: a psychiatrist in Delhi records Arjun's diagnosis with an ICD code, while a research paper on patients like him cites DSM-5 criteria; the two systems are built to map onto each other. Exam trap: 'All of these' tempts because DSM is famous worldwide, but fame in American research is not preference in Asia, and harmonisation is the opposite of contradiction. Key note: critics, including Thomas Insel of the US National Institute of Mental Health in 2013, argued that DSM categories rest on clusters of symptoms rather than biological markers, so 'based on research' is only partly true; still, it is the only true statement here, and a stands.", "topic": "DSM-5 nature and status", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about psychological disorders?", "options": {"a": "Biological approach explains these aptly", "b": "Familial approach explains these aptly", "c": "Cultural approach explains these aptly", "d": "A single approach is not sufficient to explain these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: a single approach is not sufficient to explain psychological disorders. Each classic model captures one strand of cause. The biological approach points to genes, brain structure and neurotransmitters; the psychological approaches point to unconscious conflict, faulty learning or distorted thinking; the family approach points to parenting, family conflict and expressed emotion; and the sociocultural approach points to poverty, stigma, gender roles and cultural norms. None of them alone explains why only some people with a given gene, or a given stressor, become ill. Two integrative models answer this. The diathesis-stress model, formalised by Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) as the vulnerability-stress model, holds that a disorder appears when a predisposition (the diathesis: genetic, biological or psychological) meets enough stress, and neither alone is enough. The biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) holds that biological, psychological and social factors interact in every illness. Example: Kabir has a family history of depression (biological), a habit of blaming himself for every setback (psychological) and loses his job during a family crisis (social); his depression comes from all three together, and his treatment may combine medication, cognitive therapy and family support. Option d is right because modern psychopathology treats disorders as multicausal, so no single approach explains them aptly. Exam trap: options a to c each name a real and useful perspective, which makes each sound plausible; the word 'aptly' (fully, adequately) is what makes every single-factor claim false.", "topic": "Multicausal models of psychopathology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychodynamic perspective is based on the work of:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Wilhelm Wundt", "c": "Anna O", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the psychodynamic perspective is based on the work of Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, built psychoanalysis from the 1890s onwards; The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) is its landmark. The psychodynamic perspective holds that behaviour and symptoms are driven by unconscious forces, by conflict among the id, ego and superego, by defence mechanisms such as repression, and by experiences in early childhood. 'Dynamic' refers to the push and pull of these mental forces. Later theorists revised the ideas, among them Alfred Adler (who left Freud's circle in 1911), Carl Jung (who broke away in 1913) and Erik Erikson (Childhood and Society, 1950), and the broad label 'psychodynamic' covers them all, but the root is Freud's theory. The method grew partly out of the case of Anna O (Bertha Pappenheim), a young woman with hysterical symptoms whom Josef Breuer treated between 1880 and 1882; she called her treatment the 'talking cure', and Breuer and Freud reported it in Studies on Hysteria (1895). Example: a psychodynamic therapist would read Riya's sudden dread of exams as a sign of unconscious conflict rooted in early experience and explore it through free association. Option a is right because Freud's theory and method are the foundation of the psychodynamic perspective. Exam trap: Anna O is a famous name from the birth of psychoanalysis, so she tempts, but she was a patient, not a theorist; and 'All of these' fails because Wilhelm Wundt has no place in psychodynamic theory.", "topic": "Psychodynamic perspective origins", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about social phobia?", "options": {"a": "Marked fear of one or more situations", "b": "Fear is not due to a medical condition", "c": "Social Situations always provoke anxiety", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe social phobia. Social phobia, called social anxiety disorder in DSM-5 (2013), is an intense, lasting fear of social or performance situations in which the person may be watched and judged, such as speaking in class, eating in front of others or meeting strangers. The core fear is of acting in a way, or showing anxiety signs such as blushing or trembling, that will be negatively evaluated and lead to embarrassment or rejection. DSM-5 sets out the criteria. Criterion A: marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations involving possible scrutiny by others (option a). Criterion C: the social situations almost always provoke fear or anxiety (option c); by criterion D they are avoided or endured with intense fear or anxiety. The fear is also out of proportion to the actual threat, lasts typically 6 months or more and causes clinically significant distress or impairment. Finally, criterion H says it is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance or to another medical condition, and criterion J adds that if a medical condition such as Parkinson's disease, obesity or disfigurement from burns or injury is present, the fear is clearly unrelated to it or is excessive (option b). Example: Arjun dreads college presentations, feels his face burn whenever classmates look at him and has skipped the canteen for a year; every listed feature applies. Option d is right because a, b and c are each a recognisable DSM-5 criterion. Exam trap: students pick a alone because it reads like the whole definition, but b and c are criteria too, and the item rewards seeing all three. Key note: DSM-5 says 'almost always', not 'always', and option a leaves out the word 'social', so each option is a slightly loose paraphrase; still, all three are recognisable DSM-5 criteria, and d stands.", "topic": "Social anxiety disorder criteria", "fig": ""}, {"q": "OCD patients perform better on:", "options": {"a": "Sensory memory tasks", "b": "Procedural memory tasks", "c": "STM tasks", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: OCD patients have been reported to perform better on procedural memory tasks. Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits: it is learned through practice and shown in performance rather than consciously recalled. It depends on the basal ganglia, especially the striatum (caudate and putamen), and their loops with the frontal cortex, whereas declarative memory for facts and events depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves this same fronto-striatal circuit: brain imaging shows over-activity in the orbitofrontal cortex, caudate nucleus and thalamus. The finding behind this item comes from Robert Roth and colleagues (2004), who compared 27 adults with OCD with 29 matched controls. The OCD group beat the controls on the early trials of the pursuit rotor, a skill task of keeping a stylus on a spot on a rotating disc, while their verbal declarative memory was normal; the authors linked this early advantage to striatal over-activation. Other studies find people with OCD weaker on explicit memory, especially for non-verbal material, and less confident in their own memory, which is why they doubt they locked the door and recheck. Example: Kabir, who has OCD, picks up a new tracking skill fast, yet cannot trust his memory of switching off the gas. Option b is right because procedural memory is the one system here on which an OCD advantage has been reported. Exam trap: OCD is famous for memory doubt and rechecking, so 'None of these' tempts; but that doubt concerns explicit memory and memory confidence, not skill learning. Key note: the evidence is mixed: in Roth's study the advantage faded on later trials, a PET study by Scott Rauch and colleagues (1997) found normal sequence learning but with medial temporal instead of striatal activation, and other studies found implicit learning deficits, so 'better' is not settled; but procedural memory is the only option with a reported advantage, and b stands.", "topic": "OCD and procedural memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Bipolar I is to Bipolar II as:", "options": {"a": "Mania is to Hypomania", "b": "Neurotic symptoms are to Psychotic Symptoms", "c": "Hypomania is to Mania", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: bipolar I is to bipolar II as mania is to hypomania. The relation in the stem pair is 'disorder to the elevated-mood episode that defines it'. In DSM-5 (2013), bipolar I disorder needs at least one manic episode: abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy, lasting at least 1 week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment and sometimes psychotic features such as grandiose delusions. Depressive episodes are common but not required. Bipolar II disorder needs at least one hypomanic episode (similar symptoms for at least 4 consecutive days, noticeable to others but without marked impairment, hospitalisation or psychosis) plus at least one major depressive episode, and never a full manic episode; a single manic episode turns the diagnosis into bipolar I. Bipolar II is not simply the milder form, because its depressions are frequent and disabling. Example: Meera was hospitalised after a week of sleepless, grandiose spending (mania), so she has bipolar I; Kabir has had deep depressions and a few 4-day spells of unusual energy that did not harm his work (hypomania), so he has bipolar II. Option a is right because mania defines bipolar I and hypomania defines bipolar II, in the same order as the stem pair. Exam trap: option c names the same two episodes in reverse; analogy items depend on order, and 'hypomania is to mania' would map bipolar I onto hypomania, which is backwards.", "topic": "Bipolar I versus bipolar II", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Dopamine Hypothesis is most central to:", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Anxiety", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the dopamine hypothesis is most central to schizophrenia. It states that psychotic symptoms come from overactive dopamine transmission, and its evidence came from drugs. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) found that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients; Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that such drugs block monoamine receptors in the brain, which later work pinned down to dopamine receptors; and in 1976 Philip Seeman and colleagues, and separately Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind the D2 receptor. Drugs that raise dopamine activity, such as amphetamine and L-dopa, can produce paranoid or hallucinatory states and worsen symptoms in patients. The revised hypothesis splits the picture by pathway: too much dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway (ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens) drives positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, while too little in the mesocortical pathway (to the prefrontal cortex) adds to negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: Arjun's voices fade on risperidone, which blocks D2 receptors, but his poor motivation improves little, which fits the two-pathway version. Option b is right because the dopamine hypothesis was built to explain schizophrenia and how antipsychotics work. Exam trap: dopamine also matters in depression (reward and anhedonia), so a tempts; but depression's classic biochemical theory is the monoamine hypothesis of norepinephrine and serotonin, not the dopamine hypothesis.", "topic": "Dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychological testing is what makes psychology:", "options": {"a": "Pseudoscience", "b": "Positive Science", "c": "Pure Science", "d": "Subjective"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychological testing is what makes psychology a positive science. A positive science describes and explains things as they are, through observation and measurement, as physics and biology do; a normative science (ethics, logic, aesthetics) lays down what ought to be. The term goes back to the positivism of Auguste Comte (Course of Positive Philosophy, 1830 to 1842). For psychology to be positive, its subject matter must be measured objectively, and testing supplied that. Francis Galton measured sensory discrimination and reaction time in his Anthropometric Laboratory in London (1884); James McKeen Cattell coined the term 'mental test' (1890); and Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon produced the first practical intelligence scale (1905). Anne Anastasi, whose Psychological Testing (first published 1954) became a standard text, defined a psychological test as 'essentially an objective and standardized measure of a sample of behavior'. Standard items, instructions and scoring, with norms, reliability and validity, let different examiners reach the same result and let claims be checked against data. Example: two counsellors who give Riya the same standardised aptitude test should obtain the same score, which is objective description rather than opinion. Option b is right because testing turns behaviour into objective, quantitative, checkable data, the hallmark of a positive science. Exam trap: 'positive' here has nothing to do with optimism or positive psychology; it means factual and empirical, and 'pure science' is a different idea. Key note: some read 'pure science' loosely as an exact or hard science, so c has some pull; but 'pure science' usually means basic research done for knowledge rather than application, and testing is largely applied (selection, diagnosis, guidance), so c fits less well, and b stands.", "topic": "Testing and positive science", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The psychological test LNNB comprises of:", "options": {"a": "14 scales", "b": "28 scales", "c": "7 scales", "d": "3 scales"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the LNNB comprises 14 scales. The Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery turned the flexible, qualitative bedside methods of the Russian neuropsychologist Alexander Luria (1902 to 1977) into a standardised, scored test. Anne-Lise Christensen compiled Luria's procedures (1975), and Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch standardised them at the University of Nebraska (1978; manual 1980). Form I has 269 items, each scored 0, 1 or 2, grouped into 11 clinical scales: Motor, Rhythm, Tactile, Visual, Receptive Speech, Expressive Speech, Writing, Reading, Arithmetic, Memory and Intellectual Processes. Added to these are 3 summary scales: Pathognomonic (the items most sensitive to brain damage in general), Left Hemisphere and Right Hemisphere. Eleven plus three gives the basic profile of 14 scales; higher T scores mean more impairment. Form II (279 items) adds a twelfth clinical scale, Intermediate Memory. Example: a neuropsychologist assessing Kabir after a stroke reads his profile across the 11 clinical and 3 summary scales to judge how severe the damage is and which side it lies on. Option a is right because the standard Form I profile has 11 clinical plus 3 summary scales, 14 in all. Exam trap: many sources quote only the 11 clinical scales, and d offers the 3 summary scales alone; the item counts both sets together. Do not mix it up with the other famous fixed battery, the Halstead-Reitan (Ward Halstead, 1947, extended by Ralph Reitan in the 1950s). Key note: counts vary with which scales a source includes (12 clinical scales in Form II, later summary scales such as Profile Elevation and Impairment, and optional research sets of 8 localisation and 30 factor scales), but the standard basic profile of Form I is 11 clinical plus 3 summary, 14; a stands.", "topic": "Luria-Nebraska battery scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about Rorschach Test?", "options": {"a": "It is a projective test", "b": "It is based on Exner scoring system", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "The book \"Psychodiagnostik\" nullified its importance"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Rorschach is a projective test and is scored by Exner's system, so both a and b hold. Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in Psychodiagnostik (1921). It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots shown in a fixed order: five achromatic (black and grey), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be and then, in the inquiry, where they saw it and what made it look that way. It is projective because the stimulus is ambiguous, so whatever structure the person imposes is assumed to reveal their own needs, perceptions and conflicts; Lawrence Frank coined the phrase 'projective methods' in 1939. From the late 1930s several American scoring systems grew up (Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, David Rapaport), until John Exner merged their best-supported elements into the Comprehensive System (1974), which codes location, determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), content and popular responses against norms. It became the standard way of scoring the test for decades; R-PAS (Gregory Meyer and colleagues, 2011) has since followed it. Example: Riya sees 'two people lifting a pot' on Card III; an Exner-trained psychologist codes its location, human movement and popular content rather than guessing at symbolism. Option c is right because the Rorschach is the best-known projective test and Exner's system is its standard scoring method. Exam trap: option d turns history upside down, since Psychodiagnostik is Hermann Rorschach's own book, the one that launched the test. Key note: strictly the test is not 'based on' Exner's system, since it existed about 50 years earlier and Exner's is one scoring system for it, but the item treats the Comprehensive System as the standard way it is scored; c stands.", "topic": "Rorschach test and Exner", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Electroconvulsive therapy is:", "options": {"a": "A psychiatric treatment", "b": "Effective in case of schizophrenia", "c": "Also known as electric shock therapy", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: electroconvulsive therapy is a psychiatric treatment, is effective in some cases of schizophrenia and is also known as electric shock therapy. Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini introduced it in Rome in 1938. A brief, controlled electric current is passed through electrodes on the scalp to induce a generalised seizure, usually lasting under a minute. Modern (modified) ECT is given under general anaesthesia with a muscle relaxant, so the body barely moves, typically two or three times a week for a course of about 6 to 12 sessions. It is a psychiatric treatment (option a), prescribed and given by psychiatrists. Its best-established use is severe or treatment-resistant depression, especially with psychotic features or high suicide risk, but it is also used for catatonia, mania and schizophrenia, particularly catatonic or acute forms and cases that resist clozapine (option b). The popular names electroshock or shock therapy (option c) come from its early, unmodified days. The commonest side effect is memory loss for the period around treatment, which mostly recovers. India's Mental Healthcare Act 2017 bans unmodified ECT (without anaesthesia and muscle relaxant) and allows ECT for minors only with special safeguards. Example: a psychiatrist recommends a course of ECT for Meera, whose catatonic schizophrenia has not responded to medication. Option d is right because all three statements are true. Exam trap: students who link ECT only with depression reject b, yet catatonia and drug-resistant schizophrenia are standard indications.", "topic": "Electroconvulsive therapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a positive symptom of schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Alogia", "b": "Disorganized behaviour", "c": "Flat Affect", "d": "Loss of social interest"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: disorganised behaviour is a positive symptom of schizophrenia. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to normal experience or taken away from it. Positive symptoms are excesses or distortions, things present that should not be: hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus, usually voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised speech (derailment, incoherence) and grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour (silly, unpredictable or purposeless acts, odd dress, sudden agitation). Negative symptoms are deficits, normal functions reduced or lost: alogia (poverty of speech), flat or blunted affect (little emotional expression in face and voice), avolition (lack of self-started purposeful activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality (loss of social interest). Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia, with good response to dopamine-blocking drugs, and mainly negative symptoms to Type II, with enlarged ventricles and poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen's rating scales of the 1980s, the SAPS and SANS, measure the two groups. Example: Kabir, who has schizophrenia, wears three sweaters in summer and suddenly shouts at strangers (disorganised behaviour, positive), while his flat voice and long silences are negative symptoms. Option b is right because disorganised behaviour is an abnormal excess of behaviour, not the loss of a normal function. Exam trap: 'positive' does not mean good or mild; it means added. Alogia, flat affect and loss of social interest all sound abnormal, but each is a reduction of normal function, so each is negative.", "topic": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In case of psychological science the most widely used scale is:", "options": {"a": "Nominal", "b": "Ratio", "c": "Interval", "d": "Ordinal"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the ordinal scale is the most widely used scale in psychological science. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, diagnosis). An ordinal scale adds order: more or less, higher or lower, but the gaps between points are not known to be equal. An interval scale adds equal units with an arbitrary zero (temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, reaction time), so ratios become meaningful. In the same paper Stevens wrote that most of the scales used widely and effectively by psychologists are ordinal scales. Rating scales, Likert items (strongly agree to strongly disagree), rankings and most test scores only tell us who has more of a trait, not by exactly how much: nobody can show that the step from an IQ of 100 to 110 equals the step from 130 to 140 in real intelligence. Example: Meera marks 'agree' and Arjun 'strongly agree' on a stress item; Arjun reports more stress, but the distance between the two answers is unknown. Option d is right because most psychological measurement orders people on a trait without guaranteeing equal units or a true zero. Exam trap: psychologists routinely compute means, SDs and t-tests on test scores, which treats them as interval, so students pick c; but treating scores as interval is a working assumption, not proof that the scale has equal units. Key note: many statistics texts say psychological test scores are treated as interval in practice, so c has defenders, but Stevens's own classic statement, which this stem echoes, is that the scales psychologists use most widely are ordinal; d stands.", "topic": "Levels of measurement in psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The NPC is based on:", "options": {"a": "100 cases", "b": "10000 cases", "c": "1000 cases", "d": "Infinite number of cases"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the normal probability curve (NPC) is based on an infinite number of cases. The NPC is a theoretical, mathematical model, not a picture of any real sample. Abraham de Moivre (1733) derived it as the limit of the binomial distribution as the number of trials grows without bound; Pierre-Simon Laplace (1749 to 1827) developed it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in astronomical measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. Adolphe Quetelet (1796 to 1874) applied it to human characteristics. Because it describes an infinite population, the curve is perfectly smooth, bell-shaped and symmetrical, with mean, median and mode at the same point, and its tails are asymptotic: they approach the baseline but never touch it, since in an unlimited population some case can always lie further out. Its areas are fixed: about 68 percent of cases fall within ±1 SD, 95 percent within ±2 SD and 99.7 percent within ±3 SD. Real samples of 100, 1000 or 10,000 people only approximate this shape, coming closer as the sample grows. Example: when Riya plots the heights of 1000 students she gets a bumpy histogram that is roughly bell-shaped; the smooth NPC is the ideal it approaches. Option d is right because the curve is defined for an unlimited number of cases. Exam trap: a large finite number such as 10,000 sounds 'big enough', but any finite sample gives only an approximation; the defining idea is infinity.", "topic": "Normal probability curve basis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choose the odd one:", "options": {"a": "Parametric tests demand fulfillment of assumptions", "b": "Parametric tests demand adoption of random sampling", "c": "Parametric tests are distribution free", "d": "Parametric tests include t-test, ANOVA"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: 'parametric tests are distribution free' is the odd statement, because it is false while the other three are true. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as the mean and variance and rests on assumptions about the population the scores come from: the scores are normally distributed, the groups have roughly equal variances (homogeneity of variance), observations are independent, and the dependent variable is measured on an interval or ratio scale. It also assumes the sample was drawn at random, so that sample statistics can estimate population values. The z-test, the t-tests, ANOVA (Ronald Fisher, 1925) and Pearson's r are parametric. Non-parametric tests, which Sidney Siegel brought into psychology with Nonparametric Statistics for the Behavioral Sciences (1956), make few assumptions about the population's shape and work on ranks, signs or frequency counts; that is why they are called distribution-free. Examples are the Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon, Kruskal-Wallis and chi-square tests and Spearman's rho. Their price is lower power when parametric assumptions do hold. Example: Arjun's two groups have skewed reaction times with outliers; since he cannot assume normality, he switches from a t-test to the Mann-Whitney U test, the distribution-free alternative. Option c is right because 'distribution free' describes non-parametric tests, whereas parametric tests depend on a normally distributed population. Exam trap: 'odd one' items list three true statements and one false one; c is the false one because it gives the defining label of non-parametric tests to parametric tests.", "topic": "Parametric versus non-parametric tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The limits for grouping respondents are calculated on basis of:", "options": {"a": "Mean + SD", "b": "Mean ± SD", "c": "Mean - SD", "d": "Inter-quartile range"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the limits for grouping respondents are set at the mean plus and minus one standard deviation. When a researcher wants to divide a sample into high, average and low scorers on a test or scale, the common rule uses two cut-off points. The upper limit is the mean plus 1 SD and the lower limit is the mean minus 1 SD. Those scoring above the upper limit form the high group, those below the lower limit form the low group, and everyone in between is average. In a normal distribution this gives roughly 16 percent high, 68 percent average and 16 percent low, because about 68 percent of cases lie within ±1 SD of the mean. Some studies use wider or narrower bands (±0.5 SD or ±2 SD) to form extreme groups, but the logic of a pair of limits on either side of the mean stays the same. Example: on a stress scale with mean 50 and SD 10, the limits are 60 and 40; Kabir (63) falls in the high-stress group, Meera (45) in the average group and Riya (38) in the low group. Option b is right because both limits are needed, and they come from adding the SD to the mean and subtracting it from the mean. Exam trap: options a and c each give one correct limit, so each looks right; but grouping into high, average and low needs both, which only the ± form gives.", "topic": "Grouping limits using mean and SD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A teacher ranks two groups of students on basis of their marks in psychology, which of the following types of correlation is applicable?", "options": {"a": "Pearson's Correlation", "b": "Spearman's correlation", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Point biserial correlation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: Spearman's correlation applies when the teacher has ranked the students. Charles Spearman (1904) introduced the rank-difference method, now called Spearman's rho. Each person is given a rank on each variable, the difference between the two ranks (D) is found, and rho equals 1 minus 6 times the sum of D squared, divided by N times (N squared minus 1). Like Pearson's r it runs from minus 1 to plus 1, but it uses order alone, so it suits ordinal data, relationships that rise or fall steadily without being straight lines, and data with outliers. Pearson's product-moment r (Karl Pearson, 1896) needs interval or ratio scores and a roughly linear relationship. In this stem the marks have been turned into ranks, so the data being correlated are ordinal and the proper coefficient is Spearman's rho. Kendall's tau (Maurice Kendall, 1938) is the other rank coefficient, useful for small samples with many tied ranks. Option b is right because ranked data call for a rank-order coefficient, and Spearman's rho is the standard one. Exam trap: the stem mentions marks, which are continuous scores and might suggest Pearson; but the teacher ranks the students, and once the data are ranks Pearson is no longer the proper choice (Pearson's formula applied to untied ranks simply gives rho).", "topic": "Spearman rank correlation", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Case study is best suited in:", "options": {"a": "Clinical settings", "b": "Educational Settings", "c": "Non-Clinical settings", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the case study is best suited to clinical settings. A case study is an intensive, in-depth study of one person (or a single family, group or institution) that draws on many sources: interviews, life history, observation, records and tests. It is idiographic, aiming to understand the individual case fully rather than to find laws across many people. The method was born in clinical work. Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis from case studies such as Anna O (with Josef Breuer, 1895), Dora (1905) and Little Hans (1909); John Harlow (1868) described Phineas Gage's change of personality after an iron rod passed through his frontal lobe; and William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported H.M., whose amnesia after hippocampal surgery transformed memory research. In clinical practice the case history and case formulation guide diagnosis and treatment planning, and case studies are ideal for rare disorders and for generating hypotheses. Their weaknesses are poor generalisability, no control over variables and the risk of the researcher's bias. Example: a clinical psychologist studies Riya, who has a rare dissociative disorder, through interviews with her family, school records and repeated testing, then plans therapy from this full picture. Option a is right because the case study's depth and individual focus fit diagnosis and treatment in clinical settings, where the method began. Exam trap: case studies are also used in schools (the guidance case study of one pupil) and in business teaching, so b and c tempt; but the method's classic home and best fit is clinical.", "topic": "Case study method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about Multi-trait multi method matrix?", "options": {"a": "It is an approach to examine reliability", "b": "It was given by Campbell & Fiske", "c": "It is an approach to measure construct validity", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the multitrait-multimethod matrix is an approach to measuring construct validity. Construct validity asks whether a test really measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure, such as anxiety or resilience. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) set out the idea, and Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) gave a practical way to test it. They measured two or more traits, each by two or more methods (for example self-report, peer rating and observation), and arranged all the intercorrelations in one matrix. Two kinds of evidence appear. Convergent validity: the same trait measured by different methods (monotrait-heteromethod values) should correlate highly. Discriminant validity: different traits should correlate weakly, even when measured by the same method (heterotrait-monomethod values). When different traits correlate highly just because both were self-reported, the matrix exposes method variance. Reliability coefficients sit on the main diagonal as a baseline. Example: Kabir tests a new self-esteem scale by also rating self-esteem through teacher reports, and measures anxiety both ways; the two self-esteem measures should agree with each other more than self-esteem agrees with anxiety. Option c is right because the matrix exists to examine construct validity through convergent and discriminant evidence. Exam trap: reliability values do appear in the matrix (on its diagonal), so a tempts, but they serve only as a yardstick; the purpose is validity. Key note: b is also a true statement, since the matrix was indeed given by Campbell and Fiske, so the item has two correct options and no 'both' choice; c is used because it states what the matrix is and does, while b only names its authors, and c stands.", "topic": "Multitrait-multimethod matrix", "fig": "Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske"}, {"q": "Which of the following reflects a socio emotional process?", "options": {"a": "A mother's affectionate gesture towards her son", "b": "Sorrow of a cricket captain after losing a match", "c": "Having sympathy towards beggars", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: a mother's affection, a captain's sorrow and sympathy for beggars are all socioemotional processes. Life-span developmentalists such as John Santrock, in his widely used Life-Span Development textbook, divide development into three intertwined kinds of process. Biological processes are changes in the body: genes, brain growth, height, motor skills and puberty. Cognitive processes are changes in thinking, memory, intelligence and language. Socioemotional processes are changes in a person's relationships with other people, in emotions and in personality. Now test each option. A mother's affectionate gesture towards her son is part of a relationship: the warmth exchanged between parent and child is the stuff of attachment (John Bowlby, 1969), so it is socioemotional. The cricket captain's sorrow after a defeat is an emotion, felt and shown in a social setting, so it qualifies. Sympathy towards beggars is an other-oriented emotion, concern for someone else's distress, which also shapes how we treat people; it too is socioemotional. Because each option fits, the full answer is all of these. Option d is right because socioemotional processes cover relationships, emotions and personality together, and the three options sample exactly those areas. Exam trap: examiners slip a cognitive or biological example into such a list to break 'all of these', such as a child solving a puzzle (cognitive) or a baby's first steps (biological). Ask whether the example is about feeling or relating to people; if it is, it is socioemotional. Remember too that the three processes interact: a baby's smile at her mother (socioemotional) depends on brain maturity (biological) and on recognising the mother's face (cognitive).", "topic": "Socioemotional processes in development", "fig": "John Santrock"}, {"q": "Babinski disappears in:", "options": {"a": "3 to 6 months", "b": "8-12 months", "c": "12-14 months", "d": "15-18 months"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Babinski reflex normally disappears at about 8 to 12 months of age. A reflex is an automatic, unlearned response to a specific stimulus. In the Babinski reflex, named after the French neurologist Joseph Babinski (1896), stroking the outer edge of the sole from heel towards the toes makes the big toe bend upward while the other toes fan out. Newborn reflexes are run by the brainstem and spinal cord. As the corticospinal tract (the pathway from the motor cortex to the spinal cord) becomes myelinated during the first year, the cortex takes control and the response changes to the adult plantar response: the toes curl down. Developmental textbooks such as John Santrock's therefore list the Babinski as disappearing after 9 months to 1 year. Example: when the doctor strokes 6-month-old Meera's sole, her big toe goes up; by her second birthday the same stroke makes her toes curl down. Option b is right because 8 to 12 months is the standard developmental textbook window for this reflex to disappear. Exam trap: the newborn reflex and the adult sign share a name. An upgoing big toe in an adult is the Babinski sign, a marker of upper motor neuron damage (after a stroke or spinal cord injury), not a normal reflex. Also do not confuse it with reflexes that fade earlier: rooting at about 3 to 4 months, the palmar grasp at about 3 to 6 months and the Moro (startle) at about 4 to 6 months. Key note: paediatric and neurology sources give a wider normal range, accepting an upgoing toe up to 12 to 24 months, so c is not absurd; but the developmental-psychology textbook figure is about 9 to 12 months, and b stands.", "topic": "Babinski reflex timing", "fig": "Joseph Babinski"}, {"q": "The environment where the child directly interacts with social agents is called:", "options": {"a": "Microsystem", "b": "Mesosystem", "c": "Exosystem", "d": "Macrosystem"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the microsystem is the setting in which the child interacts directly, face to face, with social agents. Urie Bronfenbrenner (The Ecology of Human Development, 1979) argued that a child develops inside a set of nested environmental systems, like Russian dolls, each influencing the child and influenced by the child in turn. The microsystem is the innermost layer: the settings the child actually takes part in, such as family, school, peer group and neighbourhood, where parents, teachers, siblings and friends act on the child directly. Influence runs both ways: a calm baby draws calm parenting, while a fussy one may strain it. The mesosystem is the set of links between microsystems, such as how home and school connect. The exosystem holds settings the child is not part of that still affect the child, such as a parent's workplace. The macrosystem is the overarching culture, with its values, laws and economy. He later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time and life transitions. Example: Arjun's microsystems are his home, his class with Ms Rao and his cricket team; a parent-teacher meeting about his marks belongs to the mesosystem; his mother's night shifts belong to the exosystem. Option a is right because 'directly interacts with social agents' is the textbook definition of the microsystem. Exam trap: 'mesosystem' tempts students because 'meso' sounds like the middle, social layer, but the mesosystem is a relationship between settings, not a setting where the child meets people. Count the layers from the child outward: micro, meso, exo, macro, then chrono for time.", "topic": "Bronfenbrenner's microsystem", "fig": "Urie Bronfenbrenner"}, {"q": "Child saying \"The road hurt me\" exemplifies:", "options": {"a": "Animism", "b": "Ethnocentricism", "c": "Centration", "d": "Intuitive thought"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: a child saying 'The road hurt me' is an example of animism. Jean Piaget (The Child's Conception of the World, 1929) found that children in the preoperational stage, roughly 2 to 7 years, readily treat lifeless objects as if they were alive, with feelings, wishes and intentions. The child who trips blames the road for hurting him on purpose, says the moon follows him on a walk, or scolds a chair for bumping her. He linked animism to the child's egocentrism: the child assumes that objects experience the world as the child does. It fades gradually through the school years, as 'alive' is narrowed to things that move by themselves and, finally, to plants and animals. Animism sits alongside the other preoperational limits: egocentrism (failing to take another's viewpoint, shown in the three mountains task), centration (fixing on one feature of a problem) and irreversibility, which together explain the failure to conserve. Option a is right because the child gives the road the intention of hurting, which is exactly what animism means: attributing life and purpose to a lifeless object. Exam trap: 'ethnocentrism' is planted because it looks like 'egocentrism'. Egocentrism is a Piagetian term about the child's viewpoint; ethnocentrism is a social psychology term about groups. Also keep animism (giving life to objects) apart from artificialism, the preoperational belief that natural things such as mountains, rivers or the sky were made by people.", "topic": "Preoperational animism", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "A child fitting a jigsaw puzzle precisely is an indication of development of:", "options": {"a": "Gross motor skill", "b": "Fine Motor Skill", "c": "Reflexes", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: fitting a jigsaw piece precisely shows the development of fine motor skills. Motor skills are of two broad kinds. Gross motor skills use the large muscles of the trunk, arms and legs: holding up the head, sitting, crawling, walking, running and jumping. Fine motor skills are precise, finely tuned movements of the small muscles, mainly of the hands and fingers, guided by the eyes (eye-hand coordination). They include reaching, the pincer grasp (thumb against forefinger, at about 9 to 12 months), stacking blocks, scribbling, turning pages, buttoning, threading beads, using scissors and writing. Fitting a puzzle piece requires the child to grip a small piece, turn it with the fingers and place it accurately in the matching gap: small-muscle precision under visual guidance. Motor development follows the proximodistal principle (from the centre of the body to the extremities) and runs from gross to fine: an infant swings the whole arm at a toy months before the fingers can pick it up, so precise hand skills such as puzzles come well after sitting and walking. Option b is right because the stem stresses precise, hand-controlled placement, the defining mark of fine motor control. Exam trap: students sometimes pick gross motor because the child is busy and active, or treat the task as purely cognitive because a puzzle needs problem solving. A puzzle does involve perception and thinking, but the word 'precisely' points to the small-muscle control that defines fine motor skill. Reflexes are a separate category altogether: automatic, unlearned responses present at birth, not skills.", "topic": "Fine motor skills", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In context of health belief model, which of the following can determine COVID-19 preventive behaviour among people?", "options": {"a": "Self Efficacy", "b": "Perceived Benefits", "c": "Perceived Barriers", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the health belief model, self-efficacy, perceived benefits and perceived barriers can all determine COVID-19 preventive behaviour. The health belief model (HBM) was developed in the 1950s by social psychologists in the US Public Health Service, Godfrey Hochbaum, Irwin Rosenstock and Stephen Kegeles, to explain why so few people used free tuberculosis screening. It says a person takes a health action when they believe they are at risk (perceived susceptibility), that the illness would be serious (perceived severity), that the action works (perceived benefits) and that its costs are small (perceived barriers); a cue to action, such as a symptom or a news report, sets it off. In 1988 Irwin Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker added self-efficacy, Albert Bandura's (1977) term for confidence in one's ability to carry out the action. Example: Kabir wears a mask and keeps his distance because he thinks he could catch COVID-19, fears it for his diabetic father, believes masks cut spread (benefit), finds them cheap and bearable (low barriers) and is confident he can keep one on all day (self-efficacy). Studies during the pandemic applied the model to masking, handwashing, distancing and vaccination. Option d is right because each listed option is a named HBM construct that can predict whether people adopt preventive behaviour. Exam trap: students think self-efficacy belongs only to Albert Bandura's social cognitive theory and drop it from the HBM, but it has been part of the model since 1988. In Nancy Janz and Marshall Becker's (1984) review of HBM studies, perceived barriers were the single strongest predictor.", "topic": "Health belief model constructs", "fig": "Irwin Rosenstock"}, {"q": "GAS concept of Hans Selye includes the domains of:", "options": {"a": "Alarm, Resistance & Exhaustion", "b": "Alarm, Reaction & Exhaustion", "c": "Alertness, Reaction & Exhaustion", "d": "Initiation, Reaction & Frustration"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome has three stages: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Hans Selye (1936) noticed that rats given very different noxious agents, from tissue extracts to cold, all showed the same triad: enlarged adrenal glands, shrunken immune (lymphatic) tissue and stomach ulcers. He concluded that the body answers any stressor with the same non-specific pattern, which he called the general adaptation syndrome (GAS) and popularised in The Stress of Life (1956). Stage 1, the alarm reaction: the body mobilises for fight or flight through the sympathetic nervous system and adrenaline; resistance first dips (shock) and then rises (countershock). Stage 2, resistance: if the stressor continues, the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis keeps cortisol high and the body adapts, but at a cost to its reserves. Stage 3, exhaustion: adaptive energy runs out and 'diseases of adaptation' such as ulcers, high blood pressure and lowered immunity appear. Example: when Riya's father falls seriously ill, her heart races and she cannot eat (alarm); for weeks she manages hospital visits and work (resistance); after months she is run down and catches every infection going (exhaustion). Option a is right because alarm, resistance and exhaustion are the three named stages, in that order. Exam trap: 'alarm reaction' is the full name of stage 1, so an option reading 'alarm, reaction and exhaustion' splits one stage into two and drops resistance. 'Alarm' also turns up as a fake stage in lists of group development stages. In Stress Without Distress (1974) he separated eustress, beneficial stress, from distress.", "topic": "Selye's general adaptation syndrome", "fig": "Hans Selye"}, {"q": "As per World Happiness Report, 2021, the rank of India is ______ out of 149 countries.", "options": {"a": "39", "b": "139", "c": "91", "d": "13"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the World Happiness Report 2021 ranked India 139th out of 149 countries. The report is published each year around 20 March, the International Day of Happiness, by the UN Sustainable Development Solutions Network, edited by John Helliwell, Richard Layard, Jeffrey Sachs and Jan-Emmanuel De Neve. Countries are ranked by life evaluation, measured with the Cantril ladder, the self-anchoring striving scale of Hadley Cantril (The Pattern of Human Concerns, 1965): people in the Gallup World Poll imagine a ladder from 0 (the worst possible life for them) to 10 (the best possible life) and say which step they stand on now. The 2021 rankings averaged surveys from 2018 to 2020. The report then explains national differences with six factors: GDP per capita, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity and perceptions of corruption. In 2021 Finland came first for the fourth year running and Afghanistan came last; India, at 139th, was in the bottom dozen. Option b is right because 139 is India's published rank in the 2021 report. Exam trap: the question names a specific year, and ranks move every year; the 2022 report placed India 136th of 146. Also note that the ladder is a judgement of life as a whole, close to the life-satisfaction part of subjective well-being described by Ed Diener (1984), not a measure of momentary mood.", "topic": "World Happiness Report 2021", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Hope, Efficacy, Resilience & Optimism together form:", "options": {"a": "Psychological Capital", "b": "Human Capital", "c": "Social Capital", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: hope, efficacy, resilience and optimism together form psychological capital (PsyCap). Fred Luthans, a founder of positive organisational behaviour, with Carolyn Youssef and Bruce Avolio (Psychological Capital, 2007), defined PsyCap as a person's positive psychological state of development, made up of four resources remembered by the acronym HERO. Hope: the will to reach goals plus the ability to find pathways to them (from C. R. Snyder's hope theory, 1991). Efficacy: confidence that one can put in the effort needed to succeed at a challenging task (Albert Bandura, 1977). Resilience: bouncing back, and even beyond, after setbacks. Optimism: expecting good outcomes and explaining failures as temporary and specific (Martin Seligman's learned optimism, 1990). PsyCap is called 'state-like': more stable than a passing mood but, unlike a fixed trait, open to development through short training, and it is measured with the 24-item Psychological Capital Questionnaire (PCQ-24). Higher PsyCap goes with better job performance, satisfaction and well-being. Example: Meera, a new bank officer, sets clear targets and plans routes to them (hope), trusts that she can learn the new software (efficacy), recovers quickly after a bad audit (resilience) and expects next quarter to go better (optimism). Option a is right because these four are exactly the components of PsyCap. Exam trap: the three capitals sound alike. Human capital is what you know, social capital is who you know, and psychological capital is who you are and who you can become.", "topic": "Psychological capital (PsyCap)", "fig": "Fred Luthans"}, {"q": "A person who is anxious about selection of career more appropriately needs:", "options": {"a": "Advice", "b": "Suggestions", "c": "Counseling", "d": "Clinical Intervention"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a person who is anxious about choosing a career most appropriately needs counselling. Counselling is a professional, face-to-face relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a basically healthy person explore a problem of living, understand himself or herself and reach his or her own decision. Guidance, by contrast, gives information, tests and advice. Career counselling grew from Frank Parsons's Choosing a Vocation (1909), whose trait-factor approach asks people to know themselves, know the world of work and match the two. Later theories added John Holland's six personality types (first proposed in 1959, later labelled RIASEC) and Donald Super's (1980) life-span, life-space theory, in which a career expresses the self-concept. Here the person has two linked needs: a decision (which field suits me?) and the anxiety around it. A counsellor can explore interests, aptitudes and values, use interest or aptitude tests where useful, help the person weigh the options, and work on the worry itself through listening, reassurance and problem solving, while leaving the final choice to the client. Option c is right because counselling addresses both the choice and the feelings, and it builds the person's own decision making rather than handing over an answer. Exam trap: 'advice' and 'suggestions' sound helpful, but they are one-way and leave the anxiety untouched; 'clinical intervention' tempts because the stem mentions anxiety, but ordinary worry about a life decision is not a mental disorder. Keep clinical intervention for diagnosable conditions with marked distress or impairment.", "topic": "Career counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following principles a counselor needs to adhere to?", "options": {"a": "Beneficence", "b": "Justice & fidelity", "c": "Non-maleficence", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: a counsellor must adhere to beneficence, justice, fidelity and non-maleficence, so all of these apply. Ethical codes in counselling rest on a small set of moral principles. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (Principles of Biomedical Ethics, 1979) gave four: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) adapted them for counselling psychology and added fidelity, and the American Counseling Association's code now lists six, adding veracity. Beneficence means actively doing good: acting for the client's welfare. Non-maleficence means avoiding harm ('above all, do no harm'): not using techniques beyond one's competence and never exploiting the client. Justice means fairness: equal respect and access whatever the client's caste, gender, religion or income. Fidelity means faithfulness: keeping promises, honouring confidentiality and being trustworthy. Autonomy means respecting the client's right to make his or her own choices. Example: counsellor Riya keeps a student client's disclosures confidential (fidelity), refers him to a psychiatrist when she sees signs of severe depression beyond her training (non-maleficence and beneficence), and gives the same time and care to every student who comes to her (justice). Option d is right because every principle named in options a, b and c is a recognised ethical principle of counselling. Exam trap: beneficence and non-maleficence are often reversed. Beneficence is doing good; non-maleficence is refraining from harm. Fidelity is keeping trust and promises, while veracity is truthfulness; and integrity is one of the APA's general principles, not one of the four biomedical principles.", "topic": "Ethical principles in counselling", "fig": "Karen Kitchener"}, {"q": "Non directive counselling is also known as:", "options": {"a": "Permissive Counselling", "b": "Non-permissive counselling", "c": "Pro-client Counselling", "d": "Self-Explanatory counselling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: non-directive counselling is also known as permissive counselling. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) proposed that the client, not the counsellor, should direct the session, because people have an inborn actualising tendency and the capacity to understand and solve their own problems. The counsellor does not advise, diagnose, interpret or steer. Instead he or she creates a warm, accepting, permissive atmosphere in which the client feels free to express any feeling without being judged, and reflects those feelings back so the client can see them clearly. He later renamed the approach client-centred (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and then person-centred, and in 1957 set out its core conditions: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). Indian guidance texts call it permissive counselling because of the freedom it gives the client. Example: Kabir starts talking about exam pressure and drifts to his father's expectations; the counsellor follows, saying 'You feel you can never quite satisfy him', rather than turning him back to study tips. Option a is right because 'permissive' captures the client's freedom to lead and to express feelings, the defining mark of the non-directive method. Exam trap: the other labels belong to its rival or to nothing at all. Directive counselling (E. G. Williamson, How to Counsel Students, 1939; trait-factor, Minnesota) is counsellor-centred and is also called prescriptive; eclectic counselling (Frederick Thorne, 1950) mixes the two. Correct alternative names for the non-directive approach are client-centred, person-centred and Rogerian.", "topic": "Non-directive (permissive) counselling", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Which of the following does not fall under the skills of counsellor?", "options": {"a": "Reflection", "b": "Active Listening", "c": "Manipulation", "d": "Accepting the feelings of client"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: manipulation is not a skill of a counsellor. Counselling skills are learned ways of helping a client explore and resolve a problem. Allen Ivey's microcounselling (late 1960s) teaches them one at a time: attending behaviour (eye contact, posture, tone, following the client's topic), then the basic listening sequence of open and closed questions, encouragers, paraphrasing, reflection of feeling and summarising. Gerard Egan (The Skilled Helper, 1975) adds empathy and challenge. Reflection mirrors back the feeling or meaning behind the client's words ('You sound let down by your friends'). Active listening, described by Carl Rogers and Richard Farson in a 1957 paper of that name, means full attention to words, tone and body language, with responses that show understanding. Accepting the client's feelings is Carl Rogers's unconditional positive regard: valuing the person without judging what he or she feels. Manipulation is steering a person covertly, by pressure, flattery, deceit or guilt, towards ends the counsellor has chosen. It violates the client's autonomy and trust and is an ethical breach, not a skill. Example: when Arjun says he wants to leave engineering for music, his counsellor reflects his excitement and fear and helps him weigh both paths; a manipulative counsellor would quietly play on his guilt about his parents to push him back. Option c is right because it is the only option that serves the counsellor's agenda instead of the client's growth. Exam trap: do not confuse manipulation with legitimate influencing skills such as confrontation (gently pointing out discrepancies), interpretation or giving information, which are open, serve the client and form part of Allen Ivey's hierarchy.", "topic": "Counselling skills", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Successful people usually work on:", "options": {"a": "Very Challenging Tasks", "b": "Less Challenging Tasks", "c": "Moderately Challenging tasks", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: successful, high-achieving people usually work on moderately challenging tasks. David McClelland (The Achievement Motive, 1953) measured the need for achievement (nAch), a learned desire to meet standards of excellence, by scoring the stories people tell about Thematic Apperception Test pictures. High scorers prefer moderate risks, want personal responsibility for results and seek quick feedback; in The Achieving Society (1961) he linked national levels of nAch to economic growth. John Atkinson (1957) explained the preference with his risk-taking model: the tendency to approach success equals the motive to succeed times the probability of success times the incentive value of success, and the incentive is 1 minus the probability (an easy win means little). The product of probability and incentive peaks when success is about 50 percent likely, that is, at moderate difficulty. In ring-toss studies (John Atkinson and George Litwin, 1960), people high in achievement motivation stood at middle distances from the peg, not right next to it or far away. Example: Riya, a high achiever, chooses a project her teacher calls hard but doable, rather than a trivial one or an almost impossible one. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives a real chance of success and a real sense of accomplishment. Exam trap: very easy and very hard tasks are the choices of people driven by fear of failure: an easy task makes failure unlikely, and an impossible one makes failure excusable. Do not confuse this with Edwin Locke's goal-setting theory (1968), in which specific, difficult goals raise performance.", "topic": "Achievement motivation and task difficulty", "fig": "David McClelland"}, {"q": "As per Maslow, Peak & Plateau experiences are:", "options": {"a": "Same", "b": "Different", "c": "Hypothetical", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: for Abraham Maslow, peak and plateau experiences are different. Abraham Maslow (Toward a Psychology of Being, 1962; Religions, Values and Peak-Experiences, 1964) studied self-actualising people and collected reports of peak experiences: sudden, brief, intense moments of ecstasy, wonder and awe in which the sense of self is transcended and the person feels at one with the world, as during a breathtaking sunrise, a creative breakthrough, love or music. They come unbidden and pass quickly. Late in life, notably in his preface (1970) to a new edition of Religions, Values and Peak-Experiences, he described the plateau experience: a calmer, serene and longer-lasting state of seeing the sacred or miraculous in ordinary things. It is less emotional and more cognitive than a peak, it can be cultivated and entered voluntarily, and it tends to come with maturity. Example: Kabir's peak experience is the instant he stands on a Himalayan summit at dawn, overwhelmed with awe; his plateau experience is the quiet, lasting sense of wonder he now feels watching his garden every morning. Option b is right because the two differ in intensity, duration and voluntariness, though both belong to healthy, growth-oriented living. Exam trap: 'plateau' here has nothing to do with the plateau phase of the sexual response cycle (William Masters and Virginia Johnson, 1966) or the flat stretch of a learning curve. 'Hypothetical' is a trap too: Abraham Maslow built both concepts from people's real accounts of their experiences, not from speculation.", "topic": "Maslow's peak and plateau experiences", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "As per Herzberg's two factor theory, the hygiene factors:", "options": {"a": "Increase employee job satisfaction", "b": "Sometimes increase, sometimes decrease employee job satisfaction", "c": "Decrease employee job satisfaction", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: in Frederick Herzberg's two-factor theory, hygiene factors do none of the things listed to job satisfaction. Frederick Herzberg, with Bernard Mausner and Barbara Snyderman (The Motivation to Work, 1959), asked about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh to describe times they had felt exceptionally good or bad about their jobs (the critical incident method). The good stories centred on motivators: achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth (job content). The bad stories centred on hygiene factors: company policy and administration, supervision, salary, interpersonal relations, working conditions, status and job security (job context). His central claim is that satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one line but two separate scales. Motivators move people from no satisfaction to satisfaction. Hygiene factors move people from dissatisfaction to no dissatisfaction: when poor they cause dissatisfaction, and when good they merely remove it, without creating satisfaction. Example: when Arjun's company raises his pay and fixes the broken air conditioning, his grumbling stops, but he feels no more engaged; that comes only when his manager gives him charge of a project and recognises his work. Option d is right because hygiene factors act on dissatisfaction, not on satisfaction, so a, b and c all misdescribe them. Exam trap: hygiene factors are also called maintenance or extrinsic factors, and salary is a hygiene factor, not a motivator, even though money feels motivating. Key note: some textbooks loosely say that poor hygiene factors 'decrease satisfaction', so c has defenders, but in this model hygiene factors act on dissatisfaction, a scale separate from satisfaction; d stands.", "topic": "Herzberg's hygiene factors", "fig": "Frederick Herzberg"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important component of an organization?", "options": {"a": "People", "b": "Technology", "c": "Structure", "d": "Environment"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: people are the most important component of an organisation. An organisation is a social system: a group of people working together, in a planned structure, towards shared goals. Organisational behaviour texts such as Keith Davis and John Newstrom's Human Behavior at Work name four core elements: people, structure, technology and environment. People make up the internal social system; structure defines their formal roles and relationships; technology gives them tools and resources; and the environment (government, markets, competitors, culture) surrounds the whole. Harold Leavitt (1965) drew a similar diamond of people, task, structure and technology and argued that changing any one changes the others. People come first because the other three exist for and through them: people design the structure, choose and run the technology, and read and adapt to the environment, and organisations are formed to serve human needs. The human relations movement that grew out of the Hawthorne studies made the same point, as did Rensis Likert (The Human Organization, 1967), who argued that human assets are a firm's most valuable resource. Example: Riya's software start-up keeps its laptops, office and reporting chart, but when its senior developers resign, the work stops until new people are hired. Option a is right because without people an organisation has no purpose, no decisions and no work done. Exam trap: technology can look most important because it is the most visible or most expensive part, and structure because it appears on the organisation chart, but both are tools that people create and use. Key note: which component matters most is partly a judgement, and systems views such as Harold Leavitt's diamond stress that the elements depend on one another, so b or c could be argued; but organisational behaviour texts treat people as the central element, and a stands.", "topic": "Components of an organisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"When workers know people are concerned about them, their productivity increases\" demonstrates:", "options": {"a": "Glass ceiling effect", "b": "Hawthorne effect", "c": "Framing Effect", "d": "Anchoring effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: productivity rising because workers know people are concerned about them demonstrates the Hawthorne effect. Between 1924 and 1932, studies at the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago tried to find how working conditions affected output. In the illumination experiments, output rose when lighting was increased, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the light was dimmed. In the relay assembly test room, a small group of women kept producing more through many changes in rest pauses and working hours. The researchers associated with Elton Mayo (1880 to 1949), whose work Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson reported in Management and the Worker (1939), concluded that the workers were responding to the attention, interest and concern shown to them, and to the close-knit group they formed, more than to the physical changes. Henry Landsberger (1958) made the name Hawthorne effect famous (John R. P. French first used it in print in 1953): people change their behaviour because they know they are being observed and attended to. The studies launched the human relations movement in management, which stressed social needs, morale and informal groups. In research, the effect is a threat to validity, controlled by giving comparison groups equal attention. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the Hawthorne finding: concern and attention, not the working conditions themselves, raised productivity. Exam trap: do not confuse it with the Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968), where others' expectations raise performance, or with demand characteristics (Martin Orne, 1962), cues that let participants guess the hypothesis. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend.", "topic": "Hawthorne effect", "fig": "Elton Mayo"}, {"q": "An organization can usually have a:", "options": {"a": "Tall structure", "b": "Flat Structure", "c": "Both (A) & (B)", "d": "Non-hierarchical structure"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: an organisation can usually have either a tall or a flat structure. Organisational structure is the formal pattern of roles, levels of authority and reporting lines. A main dimension is the span of control: the number of people who report directly to one manager. A tall structure has many levels of management and narrow spans, so each manager supervises a few people closely. It allows tight control, clear chains of command and many steps for promotion, but communication is slow and decisions travel up and down many layers. Armies, government departments and large public-sector banks are typical. A flat structure has few levels and wide spans, so each manager oversees many people. Communication is fast, employees have more autonomy and costs are lower, but managers can be overloaded and promotion paths are short. Start-ups and small firms are typical. Tom Burns and G. M. Stalker (The Management of Innovation, 1961) linked such choices to the environment: rigid, mechanistic organisations suit stable settings, while flexible, organic ones suit changing settings. Example: Kabir's 25-person design studio, where everyone reports to two partners, is flat; his mother's bank, with clerk, officer, branch manager, regional manager and head office above, is tall. Option c is right because both tall and flat structures are common, legitimate designs, so either may be found. Exam trap: students reverse the spans. Tall means narrow span, flat means wide span. And 'flat' does not mean 'without hierarchy': even a flat firm keeps some levels of authority.", "topic": "Tall and flat structures", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following types of leadership involves giving of both rewards & punishments?", "options": {"a": "Transformational Leadership", "b": "Charismatic Leadership", "c": "Transactional Leadership", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: transactional leadership involves giving both rewards and punishments. James MacGregor Burns (Leadership, 1978) distinguished transactional leaders, who lead through an exchange with followers, from transforming leaders, who raise followers' motivation and values. Bernard Bass (Leadership and Performance Beyond Expectations, 1985) developed the idea into a fuller model. Transactional leadership has two main parts. Contingent reward: the leader sets targets and gives rewards (pay, bonus, praise, promotion) when they are met. Management by exception: the leader watches for mistakes and takes corrective action, including penalties, when standards are missed; in the active form the leader monitors and corrects early, and in the passive form steps in only after problems arise. So the follower's effort is bought with rewards and kept on track with sanctions. Transformational leadership, by contrast, works through the four Is: idealised influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation and individualised consideration. Example: Arjun, a sales manager, pays a bonus to every executive who meets the monthly target and cuts the incentive of those who miss it: a transaction. Option c is right because the reward-and-punishment exchange is the defining feature of transactional leadership. Exam trap: transactional and transformational sound alike. Transactional exchanges rewards for performance; transformational inspires people beyond self-interest. Bernard Bass argued that the best leaders use both, with transformational behaviour adding to the effects of the transactional exchange.", "topic": "Transactional leadership", "fig": "James MacGregor Burns"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2021": [{"q": "The statement \"Psychology is the positive science of behaviour\" is attributed to:", "concept": "The answer is b: the definition of psychology as \"the positive science of behaviour\" is attributed to John B. Watson (1913). In his paper \"Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It\" (1913), Watson launched behaviourism by throwing out consciousness and introspection. He called psychology \"a purely objective experimental branch of natural science\", whose subject matter is behaviour and whose theoretical goal is the prediction and control of behaviour. \"Positive\" carries the sense made popular by Auguste Comte (1830 to 1842): a positive science describes what is, from observed facts, while a normative science (ethics, logic, aesthetics) says what ought to be. For example, Riya, who counts how often a child hits classmates instead of asking him to describe his inner feelings, is working in Watson's positive science. Compare the other classic definitions. Wilhelm Wundt (1879), who opened the Leipzig laboratory, studied conscious experience by trained introspection. B. F. Skinner (1938) built radical behaviourism and operant conditioning on Watson's base; Indian textbooks quote him as calling psychology the science of behaviour and experience. Option b is right because Watson is the name textbooks attach to the shift from mind to behaviour, studied as a natural, positive science. Exam trap: Skinner is the most famous behaviourist, so students pick him, but the founding definition belongs to Watson; and Wundt's subject was consciousness, the very thing Watson rejected. Key note: the \"positive science\" phrase itself appears in William McDougall's An Introduction to Social Psychology (1908), which calls psychology \"the positive science of conduct or behaviour\" (a definition he first proposed in 1905), whereas Watson's 1913 paper says \"a purely objective experimental branch of natural science\". McDougall is not offered, so d is arguable, but Indian textbooks attach this line to Watson, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "B. F. Skinner extended behaviourism into radical behaviourism and operant conditioning, but he did not found it; the line defining psychology as a positive science of behaviour is credited to Watson.", "c": "Wilhelm Wundt defined psychology as the study of immediate conscious experience through trained introspection at Leipzig, the very subject matter that Watson's science of behaviour discarded.", "d": "None of these would only fit if the line had to be credited to William McDougall, who actually wrote \"the positive science of conduct or behaviour\" (1908) and is not listed; textbooks attribute it to Watson, who is offered as b."}}, {"q": "Mathematical Psychology borrows its methods from:", "concept": "The answer is d: mathematical psychology borrows its methods from mathematics, operations research and information science, so all of these. Mathematical psychology builds formal, testable models of perception, learning, memory, choice and decision; its own journal, the Journal of Mathematical Psychology, began in 1964. Its roots lie in Gustav Fechner's (1860) law that sensation grows with the logarithm of stimulus intensity. Mathematics supplies probability, algebra and calculus: William Estes's (1950) stimulus sampling theory of learning and Duncan Luce's (1959) choice axiom are pure probability models. Operations research, the wartime science of optimising decisions, supplied optimisation and queueing models, alongside the game and decision theory of John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern (1944). Signal detection theory came from radar engineering; Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) brought it into psychology, and David Green and John Swets (1966) wrote its classic text, which separated an observer's sensitivity from their response bias. Information theory (Claude Shannon, 1948) let George Miller (1956) measure the capacity of absolute judgement in bits, and computer science added simulation and computational modelling. For example, when Meera models a radiologist's yes or no calls on X-rays, she uses probability (mathematics), cost-weighted decision rules (operations research) and computer model fitting (information technology) together. Option d is right because each of the three fields has lent methods to the discipline, so no single option is complete. Exam trap: the name suggests mathematics alone, so a looks safe, but the field openly borrows from engineering and computing as well. Key note: \"information technology\" is loose wording for information theory and computing, but each listed field has lent methods, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Mathematics (probability, algebra, calculus) is the obvious source, as in Fechner's logarithmic law and Estes's sampling models, but choosing it alone ignores the methods taken from operations research and computing.", "b": "Operations research gave decision theory, optimisation and game theory, which shaped models of choice and signal detection, but it is only one of the three sources listed.", "c": "Information technology, read as information theory and computer science, gave bits, channel capacity and computer simulation, yet it is only one source, not the whole set."}}, {"q": "Stressing the use of a product in relation to the cognitive capacity & ability of users is a domain of:", "concept": "The answer is b: designing a product around the cognitive capacities and abilities of its users is the domain of cognitive ergonomics. Ergonomics, also called human factors, fits tools, work and environments to people; the International Ergonomics Association divides it into physical, cognitive and organisational ergonomics. Cognitive ergonomics deals with perception, attention, memory, mental workload and decision making while people use a system. It asks whether a display can be read at a glance, whether a menu overloads working memory, and whether a control panel forgives errors made under stress, as in cockpit displays, mobile app menus and medical devices. Its classic text is Donald Norman's (1988) book The Design of Everyday Things (first published as The Psychology of Everyday Things), which showed that doors, stoves and switches fail when their design ignores how people think. For example, Kabir redesigns a hospital drug-pump screen so that a tired nurse cannot confuse 10 mg with 100 mg, and groups the buttons so the next step is obvious: that is cognitive ergonomics. Option b is right because the stem is about a product's use matched to users' mental capacity, which is the applied, design side of cognition. Exam trap: cognitive psychology studies the same mental processes, so a looks correct, but it studies them in general, not as a discipline for designing products and interfaces.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive psychology is the basic science of perception, memory, attention, thinking and language; it supplies the knowledge, but applying it to the design and use of products is cognitive ergonomics.", "c": "Cognitive biometrics identifies or verifies people from brain and body signals, such as EEG or heart rhythm, that reflect their cognitive or emotional state; it recognises the user rather than designing for the user.", "d": "Quantum cognition uses the mathematics of quantum probability to model judgement puzzles such as order effects and the conjunction fallacy (Jerome Busemeyer and Peter Bruza, 2012); it has nothing to do with product use."}}, {"q": "Which of the following experiments was carried out by Jane Elliot (1968) in order to demonstrate the effects of racism & prejudice?", "concept": "The answer is a: Jane Elliott's 1968 classroom exercise on racism and prejudice is known as \"A Class Divided\". Jane Elliott (1968) was a third-grade teacher in Riceville, Iowa. On 5 April 1968, the day after Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated, she told her all-white class that people of one eye colour were better than the rest. Children with the favoured eye colour got extra privileges, while the others wore collars and were criticised; the next day she reversed the roles. Within hours the \"superior\" children became bossy and cruel to their friends, and the \"inferior\" children grew timid and did worse on classroom tasks. The exercise showed how an arbitrary, visible category can produce prejudice and discrimination, and what it feels like to be on the receiving end. It reached a wide audience through the ABC documentary The Eye of the Storm (1970) and the PBS Frontline documentary A Class Divided (1985), the title by which it is now known. Its lesson matches the minimal group paradigm of Henri Tajfel and colleagues (1971): categorisation alone is enough to breed in-group favouritism. Option a is right because \"A Class Divided\" is the name under which Elliott's blue eyes, brown eyes exercise is taught. Exam trap: \"A Class Compared\" is a made-up look-alike title; only \"Divided\" is real, and the other two options are famous studies on different topics.", "traps": {"b": "The false consensus effect, shown by Lee Ross, David Greene and Pamela House (1977) with the sandwich-board study, is overestimating how many people share our own choices and opinions; it is not about racism and not Elliott's work.", "c": "A Class Compared is not a real study; it is a distractor built to look like the real title, A Class Divided.", "d": "The invisible gorilla experiment of Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999) showed inattentional blindness: viewers counting basketball passes missed a person in a gorilla suit. It concerns attention, not prejudice."}}, {"q": "MLP stands for:", "concept": "The answer is a: MLP stands for multilayer perceptron. A multilayer perceptron is a feedforward artificial neural network with an input layer, one or more hidden layers and an output layer. Each unit adds up its weighted inputs, passes the sum through a non-linear activation function and sends the result forward to the next layer. The network learns by backpropagation of error, popularised by David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams (1986): the error at the output is passed back layer by layer, and every connection weight is nudged to reduce it. The single unit is the perceptron of Frank Rosenblatt (1958). Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert (1969) proved that a single-layer perceptron cannot solve problems that are not linearly separable, such as exclusive-or (XOR); adding hidden layers removes that limit. MLPs are central to connectionism, or parallel distributed processing (David Rumelhart and James McClelland, 1986), which models knowledge as connection weights spread across many simple units working at once. For example, Meera trains an MLP to read handwritten digits: 784 pixel inputs, a hidden layer of 100 units and 10 output units, one for each digit. Option a is right because \"multilayer\" names the stacked layers and \"perceptron\" names the unit they are built from. Exam trap: option c swaps perceptron (the artificial neuron) for perception (the psychological process of interpreting sensations); one changed syllable changes the meaning.", "traps": {"b": "Microlayer perceptron is not a term; an MLP's layers are called input, hidden and output layers, and \"micro\" describes nothing in its design.", "c": "Multilayer perception swaps perceptron, Frank Rosenblatt's artificial neuron, for perception, the psychological process of interpreting sensations, so the expansion is wrong.", "d": "Movement related perception is a made-up expansion; movement-related potentials exist in EEG research, but they have nothing to do with the MLP network."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a formula for the learning curve?", "concept": "The answer is b: the learning curve is described by the power function Y = aX^b. Here Y is the time (or number of errors) on trial X, a is the time on the first trial, and b is a negative exponent that sets how fast learning proceeds. Because a power curve falls steeply at first and then flattens, it captures the classic negatively accelerated learning curve: big gains early, smaller and smaller gains later, but improvement that never quite stops. Allen Newell and Paul Rosenbloom (1981) called this the power law of practice; plotted on log-log axes it becomes a straight line. In industry the same formula is the aircraft learning curve of Theodore Wright (1936): every doubling of output cuts the average time per unit by a fixed percentage. For example, if Kabir needs 60 seconds to type a passage on trial 1 and b is minus 0.5, he needs 30 seconds on trial 4 and 15 seconds on trial 16: every fourfold increase in practice halves his time. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) was the first to describe a learning curve, from his own memorising of nonsense syllables. Option b is right because only the power form gives fast early improvement that slows with practice. Exam trap: option a looks familiar from regression, but Y = a + bX is a straight line, meaning the same gain on every trial, which real learning does not show.", "traps": {"a": "Y = a + bX is the simple linear regression line (intercept a, slope b); it predicts the same improvement on every trial, not the fast-then-slow gains of a learning curve.", "c": "Y = a + bX + c only adds a second constant to a straight line, so it is still linear and the extra c is redundant with a.", "d": "Y = aX + t is also a straight line (slope a, intercept t); it is not a standard learning-curve form and cannot show diminishing returns."}}, {"q": "In context of linkage of motivation with brain structures, choose the correct sequence:", "concept": "The answer is a: motivation is linked to brain structures through the cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loop. Garrett Alexander, Mahlon DeLong and Peter Strick (1986) described several parallel loops of this shape: motor, oculomotor, dorsolateral prefrontal, lateral orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate (limbic). In each, the cortex sends signals to the striatum (caudate, putamen and, in its ventral part, the nucleus accumbens); the striatum projects to the globus pallidus and substantia nigra; these output nuclei project to the thalamus; and the thalamus sends the signal back to the same cortical area. The loop works like a gate, letting one plan or action through while holding rivals back. The limbic loop, running through the nucleus accumbens and ventral pallidum, receives dopamine from the ventral tegmental area and turns reward signals into motivated action, the \"wanting\" that gets behaviour going. For example, when Arjun hears the ping of a game notification, dopamine in his ventral striatum tags it as rewarding, and the loop pushes the plan \"pick up the phone\" through to action. Option a is right because it gives the true order: cortex, then basal ganglia, then thalamus, then back to cortex. Exam trap: option b uses the same parts in the wrong order, placing the thalamus before the basal ganglia; in the loop the thalamus is the last relay before the cortex, not the first stop.", "traps": {"b": "Cortico-thalamo-basal ganglia-cortical names the right structures in the wrong order: the striatum receives the cortical input, and the thalamus comes after the pallidum, just before the return to cortex.", "c": "A cortico-thalamic link leaves out the basal ganglia entirely, so it cannot be the circuit that ties reward and dopamine to motivated action; as written it is not even a closed loop.", "d": "Cortico-thalamo-cortical loops are real, but they serve sensory relay, attention and sleep rhythms such as spindles; without the striatum and nucleus accumbens they are not the motivation circuit."}}, {"q": "Which method is used to identify the areas of brain related to intelligence?", "concept": "The answer is b: voxel-based morphometry is a method used to identify brain areas related to intelligence. A voxel is a tiny three-dimensional pixel of a brain scan. Voxel-based morphometry (VBM), set out by John Ashburner and Karl Friston (2000), takes structural MRI scans from many people, fits them to a common template brain, and compares the amount of grey matter at every voxel. If grey matter at a spot rises or falls with a test score, that spot is linked to the trait being measured. Richard Haier and colleagues (2004) used VBM to correlate grey matter with IQ and found the related regions spread across the frontal, temporal, parietal and occipital lobes, rather than in a single \"intelligence centre\". This work fed the parieto-frontal integration theory (P-FIT) of Rex Jung and Richard Haier (2007), which says intelligence depends on a network linking the parietal and frontal lobes. For example, Kabir's lab scans 100 students, tests their IQ, and runs VBM to see which voxels hold more grey matter in the higher scorers. Option b is right because VBM is a non-invasive, whole-brain imaging method that can relate brain structure to intelligence scores in healthy people. Exam trap: electrical stimulation sounds like brain mapping, and it is, but it maps movement, sensation and speech at single points, not general intelligence.", "traps": {"a": "Electrical stimulation, as in Wilder Penfield's mapping of the cortex during epilepsy surgery (1930s to 1950s), evokes movements, sensations or speech arrest at a point; it maps specific functions, not general intelligence.", "c": "Cryogenic blockade cools a brain area to switch it off reversibly, mostly in animal research; it is a temporary lesion method, not the way intelligence regions have been mapped.", "d": "None of these is wrong because voxel-based morphometry is a real and widely used method for linking grey matter to IQ."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a personality trait?", "concept": "The answer is d: optimism is not one of the basic personality traits listed here. The other three options belong to the Big Five, or five-factor model, the most widely accepted map of personality traits. Researchers analysing trait words in the language kept finding five broad dimensions; Lewis Goldberg (1981) named them the Big Five, and Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1985) measured them with the NEO Personality Inventory. They are openness to experience, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism (OCEAN). Each is a continuous dimension: extraversion runs from sociable and energetic to quiet and reserved, agreeableness from warm and cooperative to cold and hostile, and neuroticism from anxious and moody to calm and stable. Optimism is different. Dispositional optimism, described by Michael Scheier and Charles Carver (1985) and measured with the Life Orientation Test, is a generalised expectancy that good things will happen. It is a narrower belief about outcomes, studied mainly in health psychology, not one of the broad basic traits. For example, Arjun may be an introvert (low on extraversion) and still be confident that his exam will go well. Option d is right because optimism is the only option that is not a Big Five dimension. Exam trap: neuroticism sounds like a disorder, so students pick c, but it is a normal trait dimension of emotional instability. Key note: optimism is sometimes loosely called a trait, but it is not one of the Big Five, which is what the other three options are; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Extraversion is a Big Five dimension, and one of Hans Eysenck's (1947) superfactors: sociability, assertiveness and positive emotion versus reserve.", "b": "Agreeableness is a Big Five dimension: trust, cooperation and kindness versus antagonism and coldness.", "c": "Neuroticism is a Big Five dimension, and an Eysenck superfactor: emotional instability, anxiety and moodiness versus calm; its clinical-sounding name does not make it a disorder."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about superego?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements hold true of the superego. In the structural model of Sigmund Freud (The Ego and the Id, 1923), personality has three parts. The id is the inborn reservoir of drives and works on the pleasure principle; the ego works on the reality principle and mediates; the superego is the moral arm and works on the morality principle. The superego forms at about age 5 to 6, when the Oedipus complex is resolved and the child internalises parents' and society's standards, so that parental control becomes self-control. It has two parts. The ego ideal holds the standards of perfection the child was praised for and rewards us with pride when we live up to them: it regulates ideals (a). The conscience holds the behaviours the child was punished for and punishes us with guilt: it regulates morals (b). Like the id it allows no compromise, and its demands run directly against the id's selfish, pleasure-seeking impulses (c). For example, when Kabir finds a wallet full of cash, his id says keep it, his superego says returning it is right and keeping it would bring guilt, and his ego works out a practical way to return it. Option d is right because ideals, morals and opposition to the id are all functions of the superego. Exam trap: the ego, not the superego, is the mediator; the superego is as unbending as the id, only in the opposite direction.", "traps": {"a": "Regulating ideals is true, since the ego ideal is part of the superego, but choosing it alone leaves out the conscience and the conflict with the id.", "b": "Regulating morals is true, since the conscience is part of the superego, but it is only one of the three true statements.", "c": "Opposing the id is true, since the superego tries to stop id impulses rather than delay them, but it is only one of the three true statements."}}, {"q": "Social behaviour encompasses:", "concept": "The answer is d: social behaviour encompasses prosocial, antisocial and emotional behaviour, so all of these. Social behaviour is any behaviour directed at other people or shaped by their actual, imagined or implied presence, the subject matter of social psychology in the classic definition of Gordon Allport (1954). Prosocial behaviour is any act meant to benefit others: helping, sharing, comforting, volunteering. John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968) studied when bystanders do and do not help in an emergency. Antisocial behaviour harms others or breaks social rules: aggression, bullying, cheating, vandalism. It is still social, because it is aimed at other people. Emotional behaviour counts too when it is expressed towards or because of others: comforting a crying friend, anger at an insult, shared joy, and catching the mood of a crowd (emotional contagion). Even the way emotions are shown follows social display rules (Paul Ekman and Wallace Friesen, 1969). For example, at a family wedding Meera helps her aunt serve food (prosocial), a cousin shoves his way to the stage (antisocial), and everyone weeps at the bride's farewell (emotional): all three are social behaviour. Option d is right because each option is one kind of behaviour directed at or influenced by others. Exam trap: students often treat social behaviour as only the friendly or helpful kind, but harmful and emotional acts in social settings are social behaviour too.", "traps": {"a": "Prosocial behaviour (helping, sharing) is social behaviour, but choosing it alone leaves out the harmful and emotional kinds that the term also covers.", "b": "Antisocial behaviour (aggression, cheating) is still social behaviour because it is directed at others; choosing it alone leaves out helping and emotional behaviour.", "c": "Emotional behaviour counts when it is expressed towards or because of others, but on its own it leaves out the prosocial and antisocial kinds."}}, {"q": "The person perception is associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: person perception is associated with both categorising people's behaviour and forming inferences about their qualities. Person perception is the set of processes by which we form impressions of other people: their traits, intentions and feelings. Yaacov Trope (1986) described two stages: first we identify or categorise the behaviour (\"she is helping\"), then we infer a disposition from it (\"she is kind\"). Daniel Gilbert and colleagues (1988) added a third step: we correct that inference for the situation, but only when we have the time and mental energy to do so. Solomon Asch (1946) showed how one inference spreads: a stranger described as \"warm\" was also judged generous and happy, while \"cold\" turned the whole impression around. Fritz Heider (1958) pictured the perceiver as a naive scientist hunting for causes. For example, when Kabir sees a new classmate lend his notes to everyone, he categorises the act as generous and infers that the classmate is a kind person. Option c is right because categorisation and inference are the two linked steps of every impression, so neither a nor b alone is complete. Exam trap: option d sounds scientific, but person perception is not purely cognitive; the perceiver's mood, motives and goals shape it too, as when a good mood makes everyone seem nicer.", "traps": {"a": "Categorising behaviour is the first step of person perception (Trope's identification stage), but on its own it stops before any judgement of the person's qualities.", "b": "Inferring a person's qualities is the second step, but it rests on first categorising what the person did, so b alone is incomplete.", "d": "Cognitive processes only is wrong because emotion and motivation also shape impressions; mood colours judgements (Norbert Schwarz and Gerald Clore, 1983), and perceivers notice what their goals lead them to look for."}}, {"q": "Choose the odd one out of the following:", "concept": "The answer is d: John B. Watson (1913) is the odd one out, because Fritz Heider (1958), Edward E. Jones and Keith Davis (1965) are attribution theorists, while Watson founded behaviourism. Attribution is the process by which people infer the causes of their own and others' behaviour. Heider, in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958), founded attribution theory: he saw ordinary people as naive scientists and split causes into dispositional (inside the person) and situational (outside the person). Jones and Davis (1965) built on this with correspondent inference theory: we infer that an act reveals a matching trait when it is freely chosen, has non-common effects and is low in social desirability. Jones later showed the correspondence bias with Victor Harris (1967) in the Castro essay study. Watson, by contrast, launched behaviourism in 1913 and banished inner mental causes from psychology, the very thing attribution theory studies. For example, if Riya decides that a classmate failed because he is lazy rather than because the paper was hard, she is making the kind of dispositional attribution Heider described. Option d is right because Watson is the only name not linked to attribution theory. Exam trap: the Davis here is the social psychologist Keith Davis, not the management writer Keith Davis (1953) of grapevine research; and the attribution name students expect most, Harold Kelley (1967), is simply absent, so do not hunt for him.", "traps": {"a": "Fritz Heider founded attribution theory with his naive psychology and the dispositional versus situational distinction, so he belongs with Jones and Davis.", "b": "Edward E. Jones co-wrote correspondent inference theory with Keith Davis (1965) and studied the correspondence bias, so he is an attribution theorist, not the odd one.", "c": "Keith Davis is Jones's partner in correspondent inference theory (1965), so he fits the attribution group; he is a different person from the management writer of the same name."}}, {"q": "The statement \"Interview board may give less marks to a particular group of people\" illustrates:", "concept": "The answer is a: an interview board giving lower marks to a particular group of people illustrates bias. A bias is a systematic tilt in judgement or treatment for or against a person or group, regardless of actual merit. Social psychologists split group bias into three parts that match the ABC of attitudes. A stereotype is the cognitive part: a belief about the traits typical of a group's members (\"group X is less capable\"), what Walter Lippmann (1922) called \"pictures in our heads\". Prejudice is the affective part: a negative attitude towards people because of their group (Gordon Allport, 1954). Discrimination is the behavioural part: unfair action against them. The statement in the stem describes an action: the board marks one group lower whatever the interviewees actually say. That is biased, discriminatory treatment, the outward face of bias. Option a is right because the statement describes unfair, systematic treatment of a group, which is what bias means, and nothing in it states a belief about the group's traits. Exam trap: students see \"particular group\" and jump to stereotype, but a stereotype is a belief in the head, whereas lower marks are a behaviour. Key note: such biased marking often rests on a stereotype, so c is arguable, but the statement describes unfair treatment, not a belief about the group's traits; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "A stereotype is a generalised belief about the traits of a group's members, the cognitive part of bias; the statement describes marking, an action, not the belief that may lie behind it.", "c": "Both would need the statement to show a belief about the group's traits as well as biased treatment; it shows only the treatment, so a stereotype is at most an unstated cause.", "d": "Cognitive dissonance, from Leon Festinger (1957), is the discomfort of holding two inconsistent cognitions, which pushes a person to change an attitude or behaviour; it says nothing about marking a group lower."}}, {"q": "Interpersonal Processes:", "concept": "The answer is a: interpersonal processes involve social reinforcement. Interpersonal processes are what goes on between people: attraction and liking, friendship and love, communication, helping, conflict and influence. Much of this runs on social reinforcement, the rewards people give each other: approval, praise, attention, a smile, company. In the reinforcement-affect model of Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore (1970), we like people who make us feel good, and even people who are simply present when something pleasant happens. Social exchange theory (John Thibaut and Harold Kelley, 1959; George Homans, 1961) treats relationships as trades of rewards and costs: we stay where the rewards outweigh the costs compared with our alternatives. For example, Riya keeps sitting with Arjun in the canteen because he laughs at her jokes and praises her drawings; his attention reinforces her approach, and her company rewards him in turn. Interpersonal processes are a core domain of social psychology, with whole chapters on attraction, close relationships, helping and aggression, and they are explained by many theories, from exchange and equity to attachment. Option a is right because social reinforcement is one of the main forces that starts relationships and keeps them going. Exam trap: options c and d are sweeping absolute claims; the topic is central to social psychology and rich in theory, so both are false.", "traps": {"b": "Saying interpersonal processes do not involve social reinforcement contradicts the reward theories of attraction (Byrne and Clore) and social exchange theory, which make rewards from others central.", "c": "Interpersonal processes (attraction, relationships, helping, aggression) are a core domain of social psychology covered in every standard textbook, so this statement is false.", "d": "They are rich in theory: the reinforcement-affect model, social exchange, equity and attachment theories all explain them, so they are not devoid of explanation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following genes is associated with aggressive behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is b: the MAOA gene is the one associated with aggressive behaviour. MAOA, on the X chromosome, codes the enzyme monoamine oxidase A, which breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine after they are released. Low enzyme activity leaves these transmitters poorly cleared and is linked to impulsive aggression. Han Brunner and colleagues (1993) studied a Dutch family whose men carried a mutation that abolished the enzyme; they showed borderline intellectual disability and impulsive aggressive outbursts, a pattern now called Brunner syndrome. Avshalom Caspi, Terrie Moffitt and colleagues (2002), in the Dunedin study in New Zealand, found that boys with the low-activity MAOA variant who were maltreated in childhood were far more likely to become antisocial adults; without maltreatment the variant carried little extra risk. This is a gene-environment interaction, not a simple gene for violence. Because males have only one X chromosome, a single low-activity copy shows its effect, so most studies are of men and boys. For example, Kabir and Arjun both carry the low-activity variant, but only Kabir, who was beaten as a child, grows into a seriously aggressive adult. Option b is right because MAOA is the gene repeatedly tied to aggression, popularly nicknamed the \"warrior gene\". Exam trap: SHANK genes also turn up in behaviour genetics, but they are linked to autism, not aggression.", "traps": {"a": "SHANK3 codes a scaffolding protein at excitatory synapses; its loss is linked to autism spectrum disorder and Phelan-McDermid syndrome, not to aggression.", "c": "SHANK2 is another synaptic scaffolding gene linked to autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability, not to aggressive behaviour.", "d": "All of these is wrong because only MAOA carries the aggression link; the two SHANK genes belong to autism research."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not the stage of group formation?", "concept": "The answer is d: alarming is not a stage of group formation. Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed studies of therapy, training and laboratory groups and proposed that groups develop through four stages. In forming, members meet, are polite and unsure, and look to the leader for direction. In storming, conflict surfaces as members compete over roles, goals and influence. In norming, the group settles its conflicts, agrees on rules and roles, and becomes cohesive. In performing, the group works smoothly and puts its energy into the task. With Mary Ann Jensen in 1977, Tuckman added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the group finishes its task and disbands. For example, Meera's college project team is polite at the first meeting (forming), argues over who will present (storming), agrees on a timetable and roles (norming), and then finishes the work efficiently (performing); after submission it breaks up (adjourning). Option d is right because alarming is not in Tuckman's sequence; it rhymes with the real stages but is a fake. Exam trap: \"alarm\" is a real stage name elsewhere, the alarm reaction of the general adaptation syndrome described by Hans Selye (1936), so it feels familiar; do not carry stress stages into group development.", "traps": {"a": "Forming is Tuckman's first stage: members orient themselves, are polite and tentative, and depend on the leader.", "b": "Norming is Tuckman's third stage: the group agrees on shared rules and roles and becomes cohesive.", "c": "Storming is Tuckman's second stage: conflict and competition for roles and influence come to the surface."}}, {"q": "The statement \"A father loves his son, but sometimes dislikes him for not being serious about studies\" exemplifies:", "concept": "The answer is b: the father's feelings show an approach-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) classified conflicts by whether goals pull us towards them (approach) or push us away (avoidance). In an approach-avoidance conflict a single goal or person has both attractive and unattractive features, so it draws us and repels us at the same time; the result is ambivalence and wavering. Here the son is one object: the father loves him (approach) yet dislikes his casual attitude to studies (avoidance). Neal Miller (1944) explained the wavering with gradients: both tendencies grow stronger as the goal comes closer, but avoidance grows faster, so the person hovers where the two cross. The other types differ in the number and value of the goals. Approach-approach is a choice between two attractive goals and is the easiest to resolve. Avoidance-avoidance is a choice between two unwanted goals, the \"devil and the deep sea\", and leads to delay or attempts to escape. Double approach-avoidance involves two options, each with good and bad points, and is the commonest in real life. Option b is right because the stem describes one person who evokes both love and dislike, mixed feelings towards a single object. Exam trap: the word \"loves\" tempts approach-approach, but that type needs two attractive alternatives, and here there is only one object with good and bad sides.", "traps": {"a": "Approach-approach conflict is a choice between two attractive goals, such as two good job offers; the father faces one son with both liked and disliked features.", "c": "Avoidance-avoidance conflict is being caught between two unwanted options, such as studying a hated subject or failing it; nothing in the stem is a pair of unwanted options.", "d": "None of these is wrong because Lewin's approach-avoidance type fits exactly: one object that both attracts and repels."}}, {"q": "The informal communication network in an organization is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: the informal communication network in an organisation is called the grapevine. Every organisation has a formal network, the official chain of command along which circulars, orders and reports travel. Beside it runs an informal network built on friendships, lunch groups and chance meetings; information on it ignores rank and spreads fast. The name is usually traced to the American Civil War, when makeshift telegraph wires hung loosely from trees like grapevines. The management scholar Keith Davis (1953) studied it and found it fast and, on routine matters, mostly accurate. He described four chains: single strand (A tells B, who tells C), gossip (one person tells everyone), probability (people pass news on at random) and cluster (each person tells a few chosen others), and found the cluster chain the most common. For example, Arjun hears in the office canteen that bonuses are coming a week before the official circular appears: the news reached him on the grapevine. Option a is right because grapevine is the standard name for the informal network itself. Exam trap: rumour is the near-miss; a rumour is an unverified message that may travel on the grapevine, but the network is not the same as the message. Note also that this Keith Davis is not the social psychologist Keith Davis of correspondent inference theory.", "traps": {"b": "A rumour is unverified information that spreads from person to person; it is content that may travel on the grapevine, not the informal network itself.", "c": "Centralised communication is a formal network pattern studied by Alex Bavelas (1950) and Harold Leavitt (1951), in which messages pass through one central person; it describes structured links, not informal ties.", "d": "The wheel is the most centralised of Bavelas and Leavitt's networks: everyone is linked only to a hub, which made it fastest on simple tasks; it is a formal pattern, not the grapevine."}}, {"q": "A leader is anyone who:", "concept": "The answer is c: a leader is anyone who identifies the problem and gives the solution. Leadership is the process of influencing a group towards a shared goal. A working definition used in management texts says a leader is the person who identifies the problem the group faces and guides the group to a solution. Both halves are needed. Spotting a problem without offering a way forward is only criticism or diagnosis; handing out answers to problems someone else has defined is expert advice, not leadership. For example, when Kabir notices that his cricket team keeps losing in the last five overs and then organises extra death-bowling practice, he is leading: he named the problem and steered the team to a fix. Ralph Stogdill (1948), reviewing trait research, found leaders only slightly above their group in intelligence; traits predicted leadership weakly, and a leader in one situation might not lead in another. No fixed IQ is required, and a very large gap from followers can even reduce a leader's influence. Option c is right because it is the only option that joins recognising the group's problem with guiding it to a solution. Exam trap: option d uses \"necessarily\", an absolute word that trait research contradicts. Key note: leadership is broader than problem solving, as in the influence-based definitions of Ralph Stogdill and Bernard Bass (1985), but among these options the fullest description is c; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Identifying the problem is only half of leading; without guiding the group to a solution it is criticism or diagnosis, not leadership.", "b": "Giving the solution alone is the role of an expert or adviser; a leader also recognises and defines the problem the group faces.", "d": "Ralph Stogdill's (1948) review found leaders only slightly above the group average in intelligence; high IQ is not necessary, and a very large gap can reduce a leader's influence."}}, {"q": "Activity anorexia is to ____ as Anorexia nervosa is to ____.", "concept": "The answer is a: activity anorexia is studied in rats, just as anorexia nervosa is diagnosed in humans. The stem pair links a condition to the species in which it is found or modelled. Activity-based anorexia was first reported by Alvin Routtenberg and Andrew Kuznesof (1967): rats allowed to eat for only a short window each day and given free access to a running wheel ran more and more, ate less and less, lost weight fast and could starve to death, even though food was offered daily. Rats on the same feeding schedule without a wheel adapted and survived. W. Frank Epling, W. David Pierce and Larry Stefan (1983) set out a theory of activity-based anorexia and proposed it as a model of an activity-driven form of human anorexia; Epling and Pierce later popularised the shorter name activity anorexia. The link is that many patients with anorexia nervosa also exercise excessively and become more restless as their weight falls. Anorexia nervosa is the human eating disorder: in DSM-5 (2013), restriction of intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a disturbed experience of body shape or weight. Example: Meera, 17, diets and runs 15 km a day while her weight keeps dropping; her mix of food restriction and compulsive exercise is what the rat wheel model copies in the laboratory. Option a is right because the activity anorexia paradigm is a rat model, and anorexia nervosa is its human counterpart. Exam trap: every option keeps 'Humans' fixed, so the item turns entirely on the laboratory animal; the classic wheel-running model uses rats, not cats, ants or dogs.", "traps": {"b": "Cats belong to other classic research, such as Edward Thorndike's puzzle boxes (1898) and David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel's visual cortex studies (1959), not to the wheel-running starvation model of anorexia.", "c": "Ants are insects with no running-wheel model of self-starvation; the option is a distractor with no basis in eating-disorder research.", "d": "Dogs are the animals of Ivan Pavlov's conditioning work (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) and of Martin Seligman and Steven Maier's learned helplessness study (1967), not of activity anorexia, which uses rats in running wheels."}}, {"q": "DSM-V is:", "concept": "The answer is a: DSM-5 is based on research. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, fifth edition, was published by the American Psychiatric Association in 2013 after more than a decade of preparation: research planning conferences, reviews of the literature, expert work groups for each family of disorders, and field trials that tested how reliably clinicians could use the new criteria. Its stated aim was a more evidence-based manual, and changes such as merging autistic disorder and Asperger's disorder into one autism spectrum disorder were justified by research reviews. Option a is right because DSM-5 was built from research evidence and field testing. The other two statements are false. In most of Asia, India included, the official system in hospitals and health records is the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10, and now ICD-11, in effect from 2022); DSM dominates in the United States and in research journals. Nor is DSM-5 contradictory to ICD: it was deliberately harmonised with it and lists ICD codes (ICD-9-CM and ICD-10-CM) for its diagnoses. Example: a psychiatrist in Delhi records Arjun's diagnosis with an ICD code, while a research paper on patients like him cites DSM-5 criteria; the two systems are built to map onto each other. Exam trap: 'All of these' tempts because DSM is famous worldwide, but fame in American research is not preference in Asia, and harmonisation is the opposite of contradiction. Key note: critics, including Thomas Insel of the US National Institute of Mental Health in 2013, argued that DSM categories rest on clusters of symptoms rather than biological markers, so 'based on research' is only partly true; still, it is the only true statement here, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "In India and most of Asia the WHO's ICD (ICD-10, now ICD-11) is the official and most used classification; DSM is preferred mainly in the United States and in research, so this statement is false.", "c": "DSM-5 was harmonised with ICD and lists ICD codes for its diagnoses, so the two systems are compatible, not contradictory, even where some criteria differ in detail.", "d": "'All of these' needs b and c to be true as well, and both are false; only a holds."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about psychological disorders?", "concept": "The answer is d: a single approach is not sufficient to explain psychological disorders. Each classic model captures one strand of cause. The biological approach points to genes, brain structure and neurotransmitters; the psychological approaches point to unconscious conflict, faulty learning or distorted thinking; the family approach points to parenting, family conflict and expressed emotion; and the sociocultural approach points to poverty, stigma, gender roles and cultural norms. None of them alone explains why only some people with a given gene, or a given stressor, become ill. Two integrative models answer this. The diathesis-stress model, formalised by Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) as the vulnerability-stress model, holds that a disorder appears when a predisposition (the diathesis: genetic, biological or psychological) meets enough stress, and neither alone is enough. The biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) holds that biological, psychological and social factors interact in every illness. Example: Kabir has a family history of depression (biological), a habit of blaming himself for every setback (psychological) and loses his job during a family crisis (social); his depression comes from all three together, and his treatment may combine medication, cognitive therapy and family support. Option d is right because modern psychopathology treats disorders as multicausal, so no single approach explains them aptly. Exam trap: options a to c each name a real and useful perspective, which makes each sound plausible; the word 'aptly' (fully, adequately) is what makes every single-factor claim false.", "traps": {"a": "The biological approach (genes, neurotransmitters, brain structure) explains part of the risk, but an identical twin of a person with schizophrenia falls ill only about half the time (Irving Gottesman, 1991), so biology alone is not enough.", "b": "The familial approach (parenting, family conflict, expressed emotion) explains some risk and relapse, but many people from troubled families never develop a disorder, and it cannot account for genetic and brain findings.", "c": "The cultural approach explains how culture shapes symptoms, labelling and help-seeking (culture-bound syndromes such as dhat), but disorders such as schizophrenia and bipolar disorder occur in every culture, so culture alone cannot explain them."}}, {"q": "Psychodynamic perspective is based on the work of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the psychodynamic perspective is based on the work of Sigmund Freud. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939), a Viennese neurologist, built psychoanalysis from the 1890s onwards; The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) is its landmark. The psychodynamic perspective holds that behaviour and symptoms are driven by unconscious forces, by conflict among the id, ego and superego, by defence mechanisms such as repression, and by experiences in early childhood. 'Dynamic' refers to the push and pull of these mental forces. Later theorists revised the ideas, among them Alfred Adler (who left Freud's circle in 1911), Carl Jung (who broke away in 1913) and Erik Erikson (Childhood and Society, 1950), and the broad label 'psychodynamic' covers them all, but the root is Freud's theory. The method grew partly out of the case of Anna O (Bertha Pappenheim), a young woman with hysterical symptoms whom Josef Breuer treated between 1880 and 1882; she called her treatment the 'talking cure', and Breuer and Freud reported it in Studies on Hysteria (1895). Example: a psychodynamic therapist would read Riya's sudden dread of exams as a sign of unconscious conflict rooted in early experience and explore it through free association. Option a is right because Freud's theory and method are the foundation of the psychodynamic perspective. Exam trap: Anna O is a famous name from the birth of psychoanalysis, so she tempts, but she was a patient, not a theorist; and 'All of these' fails because Wilhelm Wundt has no place in psychodynamic theory.", "traps": {"b": "Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and studied conscious experience by introspection; he is the father of experimental psychology, not a source of psychodynamic theory.", "c": "Anna O (Bertha Pappenheim) was Josef Breuer's patient, the 'talking cure' case in Studies on Hysteria (1895); she inspired the method but was a case, not the theorist whose work the perspective rests on.", "d": "'All of these' would make Wilhelm Wundt and Anna O founders of the perspective, which they are not; only Sigmund Freud fits."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about social phobia?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe social phobia. Social phobia, called social anxiety disorder in DSM-5 (2013), is an intense, lasting fear of social or performance situations in which the person may be watched and judged, such as speaking in class, eating in front of others or meeting strangers. The core fear is of acting in a way, or showing anxiety signs such as blushing or trembling, that will be negatively evaluated and lead to embarrassment or rejection. DSM-5 sets out the criteria. Criterion A: marked fear or anxiety about one or more social situations involving possible scrutiny by others (option a). Criterion C: the social situations almost always provoke fear or anxiety (option c); by criterion D they are avoided or endured with intense fear or anxiety. The fear is also out of proportion to the actual threat, lasts typically 6 months or more and causes clinically significant distress or impairment. Finally, criterion H says it is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance or to another medical condition, and criterion J adds that if a medical condition such as Parkinson's disease, obesity or disfigurement from burns or injury is present, the fear is clearly unrelated to it or is excessive (option b). Example: Arjun dreads college presentations, feels his face burn whenever classmates look at him and has skipped the canteen for a year; every listed feature applies. Option d is right because a, b and c are each a recognisable DSM-5 criterion. Exam trap: students pick a alone because it reads like the whole definition, but b and c are criteria too, and the item rewards seeing all three. Key note: DSM-5 says 'almost always', not 'always', and option a leaves out the word 'social', so each option is a slightly loose paraphrase; still, all three are recognisable DSM-5 criteria, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Marked fear of one or more social situations is DSM-5 criterion A, but choosing it alone ignores that b and c are also part of the diagnosis.", "b": "The exclusion rules (criteria H and J: not due to a substance or medical condition) are real criteria, but they make up only one of the three true statements, not the whole answer.", "c": "That social situations (almost) always provoke anxiety is criterion C, yet picking it alone leaves out the other two true statements."}}, {"q": "OCD patients perform better on:", "concept": "The answer is b: OCD patients have been reported to perform better on procedural memory tasks. Procedural memory is implicit memory for skills and habits: it is learned through practice and shown in performance rather than consciously recalled. It depends on the basal ganglia, especially the striatum (caudate and putamen), and their loops with the frontal cortex, whereas declarative memory for facts and events depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves this same fronto-striatal circuit: brain imaging shows over-activity in the orbitofrontal cortex, caudate nucleus and thalamus. The finding behind this item comes from Robert Roth and colleagues (2004), who compared 27 adults with OCD with 29 matched controls. The OCD group beat the controls on the early trials of the pursuit rotor, a skill task of keeping a stylus on a spot on a rotating disc, while their verbal declarative memory was normal; the authors linked this early advantage to striatal over-activation. Other studies find people with OCD weaker on explicit memory, especially for non-verbal material, and less confident in their own memory, which is why they doubt they locked the door and recheck. Example: Kabir, who has OCD, picks up a new tracking skill fast, yet cannot trust his memory of switching off the gas. Option b is right because procedural memory is the one system here on which an OCD advantage has been reported. Exam trap: OCD is famous for memory doubt and rechecking, so 'None of these' tempts; but that doubt concerns explicit memory and memory confidence, not skill learning. Key note: the evidence is mixed: in Roth's study the advantage faded on later trials, a PET study by Scott Rauch and colleagues (1997) found normal sequence learning but with medial temporal instead of striatal activation, and other studies found implicit learning deficits, so 'better' is not settled; but procedural memory is the only option with a reported advantage, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Sensory memory (the brief iconic and echoic stores; George Sperling's partial-report study of iconic memory, 1960) holds raw input for under a second (iconic) to a few seconds (echoic); OCD research reports no advantage on it.", "c": "Short-term memory (digit span, the short-term store of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin, 1968) is usually intact or mildly weak in OCD, not better, so it does not fit.", "d": "'None of these' overlooks the reported procedural advantage (better early learning on the pursuit rotor skill task); OCD's well-known memory doubt lies in explicit memory and memory confidence, not in skill learning."}}, {"q": "Bipolar I is to Bipolar II as:", "concept": "The answer is a: bipolar I is to bipolar II as mania is to hypomania. The relation in the stem pair is 'disorder to the elevated-mood episode that defines it'. In DSM-5 (2013), bipolar I disorder needs at least one manic episode: abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy, lasting at least 1 week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment and sometimes psychotic features such as grandiose delusions. Depressive episodes are common but not required. Bipolar II disorder needs at least one hypomanic episode (similar symptoms for at least 4 consecutive days, noticeable to others but without marked impairment, hospitalisation or psychosis) plus at least one major depressive episode, and never a full manic episode; a single manic episode turns the diagnosis into bipolar I. Bipolar II is not simply the milder form, because its depressions are frequent and disabling. Example: Meera was hospitalised after a week of sleepless, grandiose spending (mania), so she has bipolar I; Kabir has had deep depressions and a few 4-day spells of unusual energy that did not harm his work (hypomania), so he has bipolar II. Option a is right because mania defines bipolar I and hypomania defines bipolar II, in the same order as the stem pair. Exam trap: option c names the same two episodes in reverse; analogy items depend on order, and 'hypomania is to mania' would map bipolar I onto hypomania, which is backwards.", "traps": {"b": "Neurotic versus psychotic is an old severity split (contact with reality kept or lost), not the bipolar I and II distinction; it also runs the wrong way, since mania can be psychotic while hypomania never is.", "c": "Hypomania is to mania reverses the stem: bipolar I is defined by mania and bipolar II by hypomania, so this pairing maps each disorder to the wrong episode.", "d": "'All of these' fails because b and c do not keep the stem relation; only a does."}}, {"q": "Dopamine Hypothesis is most central to:", "concept": "The answer is b: the dopamine hypothesis is most central to schizophrenia. It states that psychotic symptoms come from overactive dopamine transmission, and its evidence came from drugs. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) found that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients; Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that such drugs block monoamine receptors in the brain, which later work pinned down to dopamine receptors; and in 1976 Philip Seeman and colleagues, and separately Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind the D2 receptor. Drugs that raise dopamine activity, such as amphetamine and L-dopa, can produce paranoid or hallucinatory states and worsen symptoms in patients. The revised hypothesis splits the picture by pathway: too much dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway (ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens) drives positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, while too little in the mesocortical pathway (to the prefrontal cortex) adds to negative and cognitive symptoms. Example: Arjun's voices fade on risperidone, which blocks D2 receptors, but his poor motivation improves little, which fits the two-pathway version. Option b is right because the dopamine hypothesis was built to explain schizophrenia and how antipsychotics work. Exam trap: dopamine also matters in depression (reward and anhedonia), so a tempts; but depression's classic biochemical theory is the monoamine hypothesis of norepinephrine and serotonin, not the dopamine hypothesis.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is explained classically by the monoamine hypothesis (Joseph Schildkraut's catecholamine hypothesis, 1965, alongside serotonin theories), which fits how tricyclics and SSRIs act, not by the dopamine hypothesis.", "c": "Anxiety is linked mainly to GABA (benzodiazepines act at GABA-A receptors), serotonin, norepinephrine and the amygdala's fear circuit, not centrally to dopamine.", "d": "'None of these' is wrong because the dopamine hypothesis is the leading biochemical theory of schizophrenia."}}, {"q": "Psychological testing is what makes psychology:", "concept": "The answer is b: psychological testing is what makes psychology a positive science. A positive science describes and explains things as they are, through observation and measurement, as physics and biology do; a normative science (ethics, logic, aesthetics) lays down what ought to be. The term goes back to the positivism of Auguste Comte (Course of Positive Philosophy, 1830 to 1842). For psychology to be positive, its subject matter must be measured objectively, and testing supplied that. Francis Galton measured sensory discrimination and reaction time in his Anthropometric Laboratory in London (1884); James McKeen Cattell coined the term 'mental test' (1890); and Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon produced the first practical intelligence scale (1905). Anne Anastasi, whose Psychological Testing (first published 1954) became a standard text, defined a psychological test as 'essentially an objective and standardized measure of a sample of behavior'. Standard items, instructions and scoring, with norms, reliability and validity, let different examiners reach the same result and let claims be checked against data. Example: two counsellors who give Riya the same standardised aptitude test should obtain the same score, which is objective description rather than opinion. Option b is right because testing turns behaviour into objective, quantitative, checkable data, the hallmark of a positive science. Exam trap: 'positive' here has nothing to do with optimism or positive psychology; it means factual and empirical, and 'pure science' is a different idea. Key note: some read 'pure science' loosely as an exact or hard science, so c has some pull; but 'pure science' usually means basic research done for knowledge rather than application, and testing is largely applied (selection, diagnosis, guidance), so c fits less well, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Pseudoscience is a body of claims that looks scientific but fails scientific tests, such as phrenology or astrology; standardised testing does the opposite by putting claims to measurement and checking.", "c": "Pure science means basic research pursued for knowledge rather than use; testing is largely applied (selection, diagnosis, guidance), so it is not what makes psychology 'pure'.", "d": "Subjective judgement varies with the observer; tests are built to remove this through standardised administration and scoring, so they make psychology objective, not subjective."}}, {"q": "The psychological test LNNB comprises of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the LNNB comprises 14 scales. The Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery turned the flexible, qualitative bedside methods of the Russian neuropsychologist Alexander Luria (1902 to 1977) into a standardised, scored test. Anne-Lise Christensen compiled Luria's procedures (1975), and Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch standardised them at the University of Nebraska (1978; manual 1980). Form I has 269 items, each scored 0, 1 or 2, grouped into 11 clinical scales: Motor, Rhythm, Tactile, Visual, Receptive Speech, Expressive Speech, Writing, Reading, Arithmetic, Memory and Intellectual Processes. Added to these are 3 summary scales: Pathognomonic (the items most sensitive to brain damage in general), Left Hemisphere and Right Hemisphere. Eleven plus three gives the basic profile of 14 scales; higher T scores mean more impairment. Form II (279 items) adds a twelfth clinical scale, Intermediate Memory. Example: a neuropsychologist assessing Kabir after a stroke reads his profile across the 11 clinical and 3 summary scales to judge how severe the damage is and which side it lies on. Option a is right because the standard Form I profile has 11 clinical plus 3 summary scales, 14 in all. Exam trap: many sources quote only the 11 clinical scales, and d offers the 3 summary scales alone; the item counts both sets together. Do not mix it up with the other famous fixed battery, the Halstead-Reitan (Ward Halstead, 1947, extended by Ralph Reitan in the 1950s). Key note: counts vary with which scales a source includes (12 clinical scales in Form II, later summary scales such as Profile Elevation and Impairment, and optional research sets of 8 localisation and 30 factor scales), but the standard basic profile of Form I is 11 clinical plus 3 summary, 14; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "28 is not an LNNB profile count: Form I yields 11 clinical plus 3 summary scales (14), and the later research sets (8 localisation scales, 30 factor scales) are separate optional add-ons, not a 28-scale battery.", "c": "7 scales is not an LNNB figure; even the clinical scales alone number 11 in Form I.", "d": "3 is the number of summary scales only (Pathognomonic, Left Hemisphere, Right Hemisphere), not the whole battery's profile."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about Rorschach Test?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Rorschach is a projective test and is scored by Exner's system, so both a and b hold. Hermann Rorschach, a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test in Psychodiagnostik (1921). It has ten bilaterally symmetrical inkblots shown in a fixed order: five achromatic (black and grey), two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be and then, in the inquiry, where they saw it and what made it look that way. It is projective because the stimulus is ambiguous, so whatever structure the person imposes is assumed to reveal their own needs, perceptions and conflicts; Lawrence Frank coined the phrase 'projective methods' in 1939. From the late 1930s several American scoring systems grew up (Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, David Rapaport), until John Exner merged their best-supported elements into the Comprehensive System (1974), which codes location, determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), content and popular responses against norms. It became the standard way of scoring the test for decades; R-PAS (Gregory Meyer and colleagues, 2011) has since followed it. Example: Riya sees 'two people lifting a pot' on Card III; an Exner-trained psychologist codes its location, human movement and popular content rather than guessing at symbolism. Option c is right because the Rorschach is the best-known projective test and Exner's system is its standard scoring method. Exam trap: option d turns history upside down, since Psychodiagnostik is Hermann Rorschach's own book, the one that launched the test. Key note: strictly the test is not 'based on' Exner's system, since it existed about 50 years earlier and Exner's is one scoring system for it, but the item treats the Comprehensive System as the standard way it is scored; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The Rorschach is indeed a projective test, but choosing a alone leaves out b, which the item also treats as true, so 'Both' is the fuller answer.", "b": "John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) is the standard scoring method, but picking b alone misses that the test is also projective.", "d": "Psychodiagnostik (1921) is Hermann Rorschach's own book in which he introduced the test, so it created the test's importance rather than nullifying it."}}, {"q": "Electroconvulsive therapy is:", "concept": "The answer is d: electroconvulsive therapy is a psychiatric treatment, is effective in some cases of schizophrenia and is also known as electric shock therapy. Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini introduced it in Rome in 1938. A brief, controlled electric current is passed through electrodes on the scalp to induce a generalised seizure, usually lasting under a minute. Modern (modified) ECT is given under general anaesthesia with a muscle relaxant, so the body barely moves, typically two or three times a week for a course of about 6 to 12 sessions. It is a psychiatric treatment (option a), prescribed and given by psychiatrists. Its best-established use is severe or treatment-resistant depression, especially with psychotic features or high suicide risk, but it is also used for catatonia, mania and schizophrenia, particularly catatonic or acute forms and cases that resist clozapine (option b). The popular names electroshock or shock therapy (option c) come from its early, unmodified days. The commonest side effect is memory loss for the period around treatment, which mostly recovers. India's Mental Healthcare Act 2017 bans unmodified ECT (without anaesthesia and muscle relaxant) and allows ECT for minors only with special safeguards. Example: a psychiatrist recommends a course of ECT for Meera, whose catatonic schizophrenia has not responded to medication. Option d is right because all three statements are true. Exam trap: students who link ECT only with depression reject b, yet catatonia and drug-resistant schizophrenia are standard indications.", "traps": {"a": "ECT is a psychiatric treatment, but choosing a alone leaves out the two other true statements, its use in schizophrenia and its popular name.", "b": "ECT does help in schizophrenia (especially catatonic, acute or clozapine-resistant cases), but this is only one of three true statements.", "c": "'Electric shock therapy' or 'shock therapy' is its common name, but picking c alone ignores that it is also a psychiatric treatment used in schizophrenia."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a positive symptom of schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is b: disorganised behaviour is a positive symptom of schizophrenia. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to normal experience or taken away from it. Positive symptoms are excesses or distortions, things present that should not be: hallucinations (perceptions without a stimulus, usually voices), delusions (fixed false beliefs), disorganised speech (derailment, incoherence) and grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour (silly, unpredictable or purposeless acts, odd dress, sudden agitation). Negative symptoms are deficits, normal functions reduced or lost: alogia (poverty of speech), flat or blunted affect (little emotional expression in face and voice), avolition (lack of self-started purposeful activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality (loss of social interest). Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia, with good response to dopamine-blocking drugs, and mainly negative symptoms to Type II, with enlarged ventricles and poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen's rating scales of the 1980s, the SAPS and SANS, measure the two groups. Example: Kabir, who has schizophrenia, wears three sweaters in summer and suddenly shouts at strangers (disorganised behaviour, positive), while his flat voice and long silences are negative symptoms. Option b is right because disorganised behaviour is an abnormal excess of behaviour, not the loss of a normal function. Exam trap: 'positive' does not mean good or mild; it means added. Alogia, flat affect and loss of social interest all sound abnormal, but each is a reduction of normal function, so each is negative.", "traps": {"a": "Alogia, poverty of speech (brief, empty replies), is a negative symptom: a reduction of normal speech output, not an addition.", "c": "Flat affect, reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture, is a classic negative symptom, never a positive one.", "d": "Loss of social interest (asociality) is a negative symptom: the normal drive for social contact is reduced or absent."}}, {"q": "In case of psychological science the most widely used scale is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the ordinal scale is the most widely used scale in psychological science. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale only names categories (gender, diagnosis). An ordinal scale adds order: more or less, higher or lower, but the gaps between points are not known to be equal. An interval scale adds equal units with an arbitrary zero (temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale adds a true zero (height, reaction time), so ratios become meaningful. In the same paper Stevens wrote that most of the scales used widely and effectively by psychologists are ordinal scales. Rating scales, Likert items (strongly agree to strongly disagree), rankings and most test scores only tell us who has more of a trait, not by exactly how much: nobody can show that the step from an IQ of 100 to 110 equals the step from 130 to 140 in real intelligence. Example: Meera marks 'agree' and Arjun 'strongly agree' on a stress item; Arjun reports more stress, but the distance between the two answers is unknown. Option d is right because most psychological measurement orders people on a trait without guaranteeing equal units or a true zero. Exam trap: psychologists routinely compute means, SDs and t-tests on test scores, which treats them as interval, so students pick c; but treating scores as interval is a working assumption, not proof that the scale has equal units. Key note: many statistics texts say psychological test scores are treated as interval in practice, so c has defenders, but Stevens's own classic statement, which this stem echoes, is that the scales psychologists use most widely are ordinal; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Nominal scales only label categories (gender, diagnosis, religion) and allow counts and the mode; most psychological measures at least order people, which is more than naming.", "b": "Ratio scales need a true zero (reaction time, number of errors); intelligence, anxiety and attitudes have no absolute zero, so ratio measures are a small minority in psychology.", "c": "Interval scales need equal units (Celsius temperature); IQ and attitude totals are often treated as interval for analysis, but equal units are assumed rather than shown, so Stevens placed most psychological scales at the ordinal level."}}, {"q": "The NPC is based on:", "concept": "The answer is d: the normal probability curve (NPC) is based on an infinite number of cases. The NPC is a theoretical, mathematical model, not a picture of any real sample. Abraham de Moivre (1733) derived it as the limit of the binomial distribution as the number of trials grows without bound; Pierre-Simon Laplace (1749 to 1827) developed it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in astronomical measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. Adolphe Quetelet (1796 to 1874) applied it to human characteristics. Because it describes an infinite population, the curve is perfectly smooth, bell-shaped and symmetrical, with mean, median and mode at the same point, and its tails are asymptotic: they approach the baseline but never touch it, since in an unlimited population some case can always lie further out. Its areas are fixed: about 68 percent of cases fall within ±1 SD, 95 percent within ±2 SD and 99.7 percent within ±3 SD. Real samples of 100, 1000 or 10,000 people only approximate this shape, coming closer as the sample grows. Example: when Riya plots the heights of 1000 students she gets a bumpy histogram that is roughly bell-shaped; the smooth NPC is the ideal it approaches. Option d is right because the curve is defined for an unlimited number of cases. Exam trap: a large finite number such as 10,000 sounds 'big enough', but any finite sample gives only an approximation; the defining idea is infinity.", "traps": {"a": "100 cases is a small finite sample whose distribution is lumpy and only roughly normal, so the NPC cannot be based on it.", "b": "10,000 cases comes close to the curve but is still finite; the NPC is the limit as the number of cases grows without bound.", "c": "1000 cases gives a fair approximation of the bell shape but remains a finite sample, not the theoretical basis of the curve."}}, {"q": "Choose the odd one:", "concept": "The answer is c: 'parametric tests are distribution free' is the odd statement, because it is false while the other three are true. A parametric test estimates population parameters such as the mean and variance and rests on assumptions about the population the scores come from: the scores are normally distributed, the groups have roughly equal variances (homogeneity of variance), observations are independent, and the dependent variable is measured on an interval or ratio scale. It also assumes the sample was drawn at random, so that sample statistics can estimate population values. The z-test, the t-tests, ANOVA (Ronald Fisher, 1925) and Pearson's r are parametric. Non-parametric tests, which Sidney Siegel brought into psychology with Nonparametric Statistics for the Behavioral Sciences (1956), make few assumptions about the population's shape and work on ranks, signs or frequency counts; that is why they are called distribution-free. Examples are the Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon, Kruskal-Wallis and chi-square tests and Spearman's rho. Their price is lower power when parametric assumptions do hold. Example: Arjun's two groups have skewed reaction times with outliers; since he cannot assume normality, he switches from a t-test to the Mann-Whitney U test, the distribution-free alternative. Option c is right because 'distribution free' describes non-parametric tests, whereas parametric tests depend on a normally distributed population. Exam trap: 'odd one' items list three true statements and one false one; c is the false one because it gives the defining label of non-parametric tests to parametric tests.", "traps": {"a": "True statement: parametric tests do demand that assumptions such as normality, homogeneity of variance and interval data be met, so it is not the odd one.", "b": "True statement: parametric inference assumes random sampling from the population so that sample statistics can estimate parameters; it belongs with the others.", "d": "True statement: the t-test and ANOVA (with Pearson's r and the z-test) are standard parametric tests, so it is not the odd one."}}, {"q": "The limits for grouping respondents are calculated on basis of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the limits for grouping respondents are set at the mean plus and minus one standard deviation. When a researcher wants to divide a sample into high, average and low scorers on a test or scale, the common rule uses two cut-off points. The upper limit is the mean plus 1 SD and the lower limit is the mean minus 1 SD. Those scoring above the upper limit form the high group, those below the lower limit form the low group, and everyone in between is average. In a normal distribution this gives roughly 16 percent high, 68 percent average and 16 percent low, because about 68 percent of cases lie within ±1 SD of the mean. Some studies use wider or narrower bands (±0.5 SD or ±2 SD) to form extreme groups, but the logic of a pair of limits on either side of the mean stays the same. Example: on a stress scale with mean 50 and SD 10, the limits are 60 and 40; Kabir (63) falls in the high-stress group, Meera (45) in the average group and Riya (38) in the low group. Option b is right because both limits are needed, and they come from adding the SD to the mean and subtracting it from the mean. Exam trap: options a and c each give one correct limit, so each looks right; but grouping into high, average and low needs both, which only the ± form gives.", "traps": {"a": "Mean + SD gives only the upper cut-off, separating the high group from the rest; it cannot define the low group.", "c": "Mean minus SD gives only the lower cut-off for the low group; on its own it leaves the high group undefined.", "d": "The interquartile range (Q3 minus Q1) underlies quartile splits such as the top and bottom 25 percent, a different, median-based method used mainly with skewed data, not the standard mean and SD limits."}}, {"q": "A teacher ranks two groups of students on basis of their marks in psychology, which of the following types of correlation is applicable?", "concept": "The answer is b: Spearman's correlation applies when the teacher has ranked the students. Charles Spearman (1904) introduced the rank-difference method, now called Spearman's rho. Each person is given a rank on each variable, the difference between the two ranks (D) is found, and rho equals 1 minus 6 times the sum of D squared, divided by N times (N squared minus 1). Like Pearson's r it runs from minus 1 to plus 1, but it uses order alone, so it suits ordinal data, relationships that rise or fall steadily without being straight lines, and data with outliers. Pearson's product-moment r (Karl Pearson, 1896) needs interval or ratio scores and a roughly linear relationship. In this stem the marks have been turned into ranks, so the data being correlated are ordinal and the proper coefficient is Spearman's rho. Kendall's tau (Maurice Kendall, 1938) is the other rank coefficient, useful for small samples with many tied ranks. Option b is right because ranked data call for a rank-order coefficient, and Spearman's rho is the standard one. Exam trap: the stem mentions marks, which are continuous scores and might suggest Pearson; but the teacher ranks the students, and once the data are ranks Pearson is no longer the proper choice (Pearson's formula applied to untied ranks simply gives rho).", "traps": {"a": "Pearson's r (Karl Pearson, 1896) correlates raw interval or ratio scores with a linear relationship; here the marks have been turned into ranks, so it is not the appropriate coefficient.", "c": "'Both' fails because the data are ranks; Pearson's r computed on ranks only reproduces Spearman's rho, so Spearman alone is the right choice.", "d": "Point-biserial correlation links a true dichotomy (group A or B, pass or fail) with a continuous score; the mention of two groups tempts, but the teacher has ranked the students, so the data are ranks, which call for Spearman."}}, {"q": "Case study is best suited in:", "concept": "The answer is a: the case study is best suited to clinical settings. A case study is an intensive, in-depth study of one person (or a single family, group or institution) that draws on many sources: interviews, life history, observation, records and tests. It is idiographic, aiming to understand the individual case fully rather than to find laws across many people. The method was born in clinical work. Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis from case studies such as Anna O (with Josef Breuer, 1895), Dora (1905) and Little Hans (1909); John Harlow (1868) described Phineas Gage's change of personality after an iron rod passed through his frontal lobe; and William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported H.M., whose amnesia after hippocampal surgery transformed memory research. In clinical practice the case history and case formulation guide diagnosis and treatment planning, and case studies are ideal for rare disorders and for generating hypotheses. Their weaknesses are poor generalisability, no control over variables and the risk of the researcher's bias. Example: a clinical psychologist studies Riya, who has a rare dissociative disorder, through interviews with her family, school records and repeated testing, then plans therapy from this full picture. Option a is right because the case study's depth and individual focus fit diagnosis and treatment in clinical settings, where the method began. Exam trap: case studies are also used in schools (the guidance case study of one pupil) and in business teaching, so b and c tempt; but the method's classic home and best fit is clinical.", "traps": {"b": "Educational settings use the case study as one tool of the guidance programme (an in-depth picture of one pupil), but there it sits beside tests, records and interviews; its classic and best fit is clinical.", "c": "'Non-clinical settings' is too broad: case studies appear in business, education and anthropology, yet the method is most suited to, and most developed in, clinical diagnosis and treatment.", "d": "'None of these' is wrong because the case study is a standard method of clinical work, from Sigmund Freud's patients to H.M."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about Multi-trait multi method matrix?", "concept": "The answer is c: the multitrait-multimethod matrix is an approach to measuring construct validity. Construct validity asks whether a test really measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure, such as anxiety or resilience. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) set out the idea, and Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) gave a practical way to test it. They measured two or more traits, each by two or more methods (for example self-report, peer rating and observation), and arranged all the intercorrelations in one matrix. Two kinds of evidence appear. Convergent validity: the same trait measured by different methods (monotrait-heteromethod values) should correlate highly. Discriminant validity: different traits should correlate weakly, even when measured by the same method (heterotrait-monomethod values). When different traits correlate highly just because both were self-reported, the matrix exposes method variance. Reliability coefficients sit on the main diagonal as a baseline. Example: Kabir tests a new self-esteem scale by also rating self-esteem through teacher reports, and measures anxiety both ways; the two self-esteem measures should agree with each other more than self-esteem agrees with anxiety. Option c is right because the matrix exists to examine construct validity through convergent and discriminant evidence. Exam trap: reliability values do appear in the matrix (on its diagonal), so a tempts, but they serve only as a yardstick; the purpose is validity. Key note: b is also a true statement, since the matrix was indeed given by Campbell and Fiske, so the item has two correct options and no 'both' choice; c is used because it states what the matrix is and does, while b only names its authors, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Reliability coefficients sit on the matrix's diagonal, but only as a baseline; the matrix was designed to examine validity (convergent and discriminant), not reliability.", "b": "The attribution is accurate (Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske, 1959), but it only names the authors; c states what the matrix is for, which is the fuller answer to what holds true about it.", "d": "'None of these' is wrong because c, and the attribution in b, are both true."}}, {"q": "Which of the following reflects a socio emotional process?", "concept": "The answer is d: a mother's affection, a captain's sorrow and sympathy for beggars are all socioemotional processes. Life-span developmentalists such as John Santrock, in his widely used Life-Span Development textbook, divide development into three intertwined kinds of process. Biological processes are changes in the body: genes, brain growth, height, motor skills and puberty. Cognitive processes are changes in thinking, memory, intelligence and language. Socioemotional processes are changes in a person's relationships with other people, in emotions and in personality. Now test each option. A mother's affectionate gesture towards her son is part of a relationship: the warmth exchanged between parent and child is the stuff of attachment (John Bowlby, 1969), so it is socioemotional. The cricket captain's sorrow after a defeat is an emotion, felt and shown in a social setting, so it qualifies. Sympathy towards beggars is an other-oriented emotion, concern for someone else's distress, which also shapes how we treat people; it too is socioemotional. Because each option fits, the full answer is all of these. Option d is right because socioemotional processes cover relationships, emotions and personality together, and the three options sample exactly those areas. Exam trap: examiners slip a cognitive or biological example into such a list to break 'all of these', such as a child solving a puzzle (cognitive) or a baby's first steps (biological). Ask whether the example is about feeling or relating to people; if it is, it is socioemotional. Remember too that the three processes interact: a baby's smile at her mother (socioemotional) depends on brain maturity (biological) and on recognising the mother's face (cognitive).", "traps": {"a": "A mother's affection is a genuine socioemotional process (a relationship and attachment), but choosing it alone leaves out the captain's emotion and the sympathy, which qualify just as well.", "b": "The captain's sorrow is an emotion and so is socioemotional, but picking it alone ignores the mother's affection (a relationship) and the sympathy for beggars (a social emotion).", "c": "Sympathy is an other-oriented emotion and counts, but it is only one of three valid examples; the mother's gesture and the captain's sorrow are equally socioemotional."}}, {"q": "Babinski disappears in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Babinski reflex normally disappears at about 8 to 12 months of age. A reflex is an automatic, unlearned response to a specific stimulus. In the Babinski reflex, named after the French neurologist Joseph Babinski (1896), stroking the outer edge of the sole from heel towards the toes makes the big toe bend upward while the other toes fan out. Newborn reflexes are run by the brainstem and spinal cord. As the corticospinal tract (the pathway from the motor cortex to the spinal cord) becomes myelinated during the first year, the cortex takes control and the response changes to the adult plantar response: the toes curl down. Developmental textbooks such as John Santrock's therefore list the Babinski as disappearing after 9 months to 1 year. Example: when the doctor strokes 6-month-old Meera's sole, her big toe goes up; by her second birthday the same stroke makes her toes curl down. Option b is right because 8 to 12 months is the standard developmental textbook window for this reflex to disappear. Exam trap: the newborn reflex and the adult sign share a name. An upgoing big toe in an adult is the Babinski sign, a marker of upper motor neuron damage (after a stroke or spinal cord injury), not a normal reflex. Also do not confuse it with reflexes that fade earlier: rooting at about 3 to 4 months, the palmar grasp at about 3 to 6 months and the Moro (startle) at about 4 to 6 months. Key note: paediatric and neurology sources give a wider normal range, accepting an upgoing toe up to 12 to 24 months, so c is not absurd; but the developmental-psychology textbook figure is about 9 to 12 months, and b stands.", "traps": {"a": "3 to 6 months is when reflexes such as rooting, sucking, the palmar grasp and the Moro fade; the Babinski outlasts them and is still present at 6 months.", "c": "12 to 14 months is later than the developmental textbook window; some neurology sources allow an upgoing toe until about 2 years, but the standard answer is that it has gone by about 12 months.", "d": "15 to 18 months is well past the developmental textbook window of 9 to 12 months; it falls only inside the wider neurology range, which is not the figure psychology texts teach."}}, {"q": "The environment where the child directly interacts with social agents is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: the microsystem is the setting in which the child interacts directly, face to face, with social agents. Urie Bronfenbrenner (The Ecology of Human Development, 1979) argued that a child develops inside a set of nested environmental systems, like Russian dolls, each influencing the child and influenced by the child in turn. The microsystem is the innermost layer: the settings the child actually takes part in, such as family, school, peer group and neighbourhood, where parents, teachers, siblings and friends act on the child directly. Influence runs both ways: a calm baby draws calm parenting, while a fussy one may strain it. The mesosystem is the set of links between microsystems, such as how home and school connect. The exosystem holds settings the child is not part of that still affect the child, such as a parent's workplace. The macrosystem is the overarching culture, with its values, laws and economy. He later added the chronosystem, the dimension of time and life transitions. Example: Arjun's microsystems are his home, his class with Ms Rao and his cricket team; a parent-teacher meeting about his marks belongs to the mesosystem; his mother's night shifts belong to the exosystem. Option a is right because 'directly interacts with social agents' is the textbook definition of the microsystem. Exam trap: 'mesosystem' tempts students because 'meso' sounds like the middle, social layer, but the mesosystem is a relationship between settings, not a setting where the child meets people. Count the layers from the child outward: micro, meso, exo, macro, then chrono for time.", "traps": {"b": "The mesosystem is the set of connections between microsystems, such as parents meeting teachers; it links settings rather than being the setting where the child interacts with people.", "c": "The exosystem contains settings the child does not enter, such as a parent's workplace or the local school board, which affect the child only indirectly.", "d": "The macrosystem is the broad culture (values, laws, customs, economic system) in which all the other systems sit; it shapes the child from a distance, not through face-to-face contact."}}, {"q": "Child saying \"The road hurt me\" exemplifies:", "concept": "The answer is a: a child saying 'The road hurt me' is an example of animism. Jean Piaget (The Child's Conception of the World, 1929) found that children in the preoperational stage, roughly 2 to 7 years, readily treat lifeless objects as if they were alive, with feelings, wishes and intentions. The child who trips blames the road for hurting him on purpose, says the moon follows him on a walk, or scolds a chair for bumping her. He linked animism to the child's egocentrism: the child assumes that objects experience the world as the child does. It fades gradually through the school years, as 'alive' is narrowed to things that move by themselves and, finally, to plants and animals. Animism sits alongside the other preoperational limits: egocentrism (failing to take another's viewpoint, shown in the three mountains task), centration (fixing on one feature of a problem) and irreversibility, which together explain the failure to conserve. Option a is right because the child gives the road the intention of hurting, which is exactly what animism means: attributing life and purpose to a lifeless object. Exam trap: 'ethnocentrism' is planted because it looks like 'egocentrism'. Egocentrism is a Piagetian term about the child's viewpoint; ethnocentrism is a social psychology term about groups. Also keep animism (giving life to objects) apart from artificialism, the preoperational belief that natural things such as mountains, rivers or the sky were made by people.", "traps": {"b": "Ethnocentrism (William Graham Sumner, 1906) is judging other cultural groups by the standards of one's own group; it is a social psychology idea about groups, not a feature of children's thinking about objects.", "c": "Centration is focusing on one salient feature while ignoring others, such as judging the amount of juice by the height of the glass; it does not involve giving life or feelings to objects.", "d": "Intuitive thought is the later preoperational substage (about 4 to 7 years) in which children reason in a primitive way and ask many 'why' questions, sure of answers they cannot justify; it names a phase, not the specific error of giving objects intentions."}}, {"q": "A child fitting a jigsaw puzzle precisely is an indication of development of:", "concept": "The answer is b: fitting a jigsaw piece precisely shows the development of fine motor skills. Motor skills are of two broad kinds. Gross motor skills use the large muscles of the trunk, arms and legs: holding up the head, sitting, crawling, walking, running and jumping. Fine motor skills are precise, finely tuned movements of the small muscles, mainly of the hands and fingers, guided by the eyes (eye-hand coordination). They include reaching, the pincer grasp (thumb against forefinger, at about 9 to 12 months), stacking blocks, scribbling, turning pages, buttoning, threading beads, using scissors and writing. Fitting a puzzle piece requires the child to grip a small piece, turn it with the fingers and place it accurately in the matching gap: small-muscle precision under visual guidance. Motor development follows the proximodistal principle (from the centre of the body to the extremities) and runs from gross to fine: an infant swings the whole arm at a toy months before the fingers can pick it up, so precise hand skills such as puzzles come well after sitting and walking. Option b is right because the stem stresses precise, hand-controlled placement, the defining mark of fine motor control. Exam trap: students sometimes pick gross motor because the child is busy and active, or treat the task as purely cognitive because a puzzle needs problem solving. A puzzle does involve perception and thinking, but the word 'precisely' points to the small-muscle control that defines fine motor skill. Reflexes are a separate category altogether: automatic, unlearned responses present at birth, not skills.", "traps": {"a": "Gross motor skills use large muscles for movements such as crawling, walking, running and jumping; fitting a puzzle relies on the small muscles of the hands and fingers.", "c": "Reflexes are automatic, unlearned responses present at birth, such as rooting, sucking and the palmar grasp; fitting a puzzle is a voluntary, learned, goal-directed act.", "d": "None of these is wrong because fine motor skill describes the act exactly: precise, eye-guided movement of the hands and fingers."}}, {"q": "In context of health belief model, which of the following can determine COVID-19 preventive behaviour among people?", "concept": "The answer is d: in the health belief model, self-efficacy, perceived benefits and perceived barriers can all determine COVID-19 preventive behaviour. The health belief model (HBM) was developed in the 1950s by social psychologists in the US Public Health Service, Godfrey Hochbaum, Irwin Rosenstock and Stephen Kegeles, to explain why so few people used free tuberculosis screening. It says a person takes a health action when they believe they are at risk (perceived susceptibility), that the illness would be serious (perceived severity), that the action works (perceived benefits) and that its costs are small (perceived barriers); a cue to action, such as a symptom or a news report, sets it off. In 1988 Irwin Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker added self-efficacy, Albert Bandura's (1977) term for confidence in one's ability to carry out the action. Example: Kabir wears a mask and keeps his distance because he thinks he could catch COVID-19, fears it for his diabetic father, believes masks cut spread (benefit), finds them cheap and bearable (low barriers) and is confident he can keep one on all day (self-efficacy). Studies during the pandemic applied the model to masking, handwashing, distancing and vaccination. Option d is right because each listed option is a named HBM construct that can predict whether people adopt preventive behaviour. Exam trap: students think self-efficacy belongs only to Albert Bandura's social cognitive theory and drop it from the HBM, but it has been part of the model since 1988. In Nancy Janz and Marshall Becker's (1984) review of HBM studies, perceived barriers were the single strongest predictor.", "traps": {"a": "Self-efficacy is a real HBM construct (added in 1988), but choosing it alone leaves out perceived benefits and barriers, which also predict preventive behaviour.", "b": "Perceived benefits (the belief that masks or vaccines work) are part of the model, but picking only this option ignores barriers and self-efficacy.", "c": "Perceived barriers (cost, discomfort, inconvenience) are the strongest single HBM predictor, yet choosing them alone misses benefits and self-efficacy, which the model also includes."}}, {"q": "GAS concept of Hans Selye includes the domains of:", "concept": "The answer is a: Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome has three stages: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Hans Selye (1936) noticed that rats given very different noxious agents, from tissue extracts to cold, all showed the same triad: enlarged adrenal glands, shrunken immune (lymphatic) tissue and stomach ulcers. He concluded that the body answers any stressor with the same non-specific pattern, which he called the general adaptation syndrome (GAS) and popularised in The Stress of Life (1956). Stage 1, the alarm reaction: the body mobilises for fight or flight through the sympathetic nervous system and adrenaline; resistance first dips (shock) and then rises (countershock). Stage 2, resistance: if the stressor continues, the slower hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis keeps cortisol high and the body adapts, but at a cost to its reserves. Stage 3, exhaustion: adaptive energy runs out and 'diseases of adaptation' such as ulcers, high blood pressure and lowered immunity appear. Example: when Riya's father falls seriously ill, her heart races and she cannot eat (alarm); for weeks she manages hospital visits and work (resistance); after months she is run down and catches every infection going (exhaustion). Option a is right because alarm, resistance and exhaustion are the three named stages, in that order. Exam trap: 'alarm reaction' is the full name of stage 1, so an option reading 'alarm, reaction and exhaustion' splits one stage into two and drops resistance. 'Alarm' also turns up as a fake stage in lists of group development stages. In Stress Without Distress (1974) he separated eustress, beneficial stress, from distress.", "traps": {"b": "This option splits the first stage, the alarm reaction, into two words and leaves out the stage of resistance, so it is not Hans Selye's sequence.", "c": "Alertness and reaction are not GAS stages; the first stage is the alarm reaction and the middle one is resistance.", "d": "Initiation, reaction and frustration are invented; frustration belongs to motivation theory (blocked goals, as in John Dollard's frustration-aggression hypothesis of 1939), not to Hans Selye's model."}}, {"q": "As per World Happiness Report, 2021, the rank of India is ______ out of 149 countries.", "concept": "The answer is b: the World Happiness Report 2021 ranked India 139th out of 149 countries. The report is published each year around 20 March, the International Day of Happiness, by the UN Sustainable Development Solutions Network, edited by John Helliwell, Richard Layard, Jeffrey Sachs and Jan-Emmanuel De Neve. Countries are ranked by life evaluation, measured with the Cantril ladder, the self-anchoring striving scale of Hadley Cantril (The Pattern of Human Concerns, 1965): people in the Gallup World Poll imagine a ladder from 0 (the worst possible life for them) to 10 (the best possible life) and say which step they stand on now. The 2021 rankings averaged surveys from 2018 to 2020. The report then explains national differences with six factors: GDP per capita, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity and perceptions of corruption. In 2021 Finland came first for the fourth year running and Afghanistan came last; India, at 139th, was in the bottom dozen. Option b is right because 139 is India's published rank in the 2021 report. Exam trap: the question names a specific year, and ranks move every year; the 2022 report placed India 136th of 146. Also note that the ladder is a judgement of life as a whole, close to the life-satisfaction part of subjective well-being described by Ed Diener (1984), not a measure of momentary mood.", "traps": {"a": "39 drops the leading 1 of the true rank; it would put India in the top third of the table, far above its actual place near the bottom.", "c": "91 would be a mid-table rank; India was in the bottom dozen in 2021, at 139th.", "d": "13 would place India among the happiest nations, a zone held mainly by Nordic and Western European countries; it bears no relation to India's 2021 rank."}}, {"q": "Hope, Efficacy, Resilience & Optimism together form:", "concept": "The answer is a: hope, efficacy, resilience and optimism together form psychological capital (PsyCap). Fred Luthans, a founder of positive organisational behaviour, with Carolyn Youssef and Bruce Avolio (Psychological Capital, 2007), defined PsyCap as a person's positive psychological state of development, made up of four resources remembered by the acronym HERO. Hope: the will to reach goals plus the ability to find pathways to them (from C. R. Snyder's hope theory, 1991). Efficacy: confidence that one can put in the effort needed to succeed at a challenging task (Albert Bandura, 1977). Resilience: bouncing back, and even beyond, after setbacks. Optimism: expecting good outcomes and explaining failures as temporary and specific (Martin Seligman's learned optimism, 1990). PsyCap is called 'state-like': more stable than a passing mood but, unlike a fixed trait, open to development through short training, and it is measured with the 24-item Psychological Capital Questionnaire (PCQ-24). Higher PsyCap goes with better job performance, satisfaction and well-being. Example: Meera, a new bank officer, sets clear targets and plans routes to them (hope), trusts that she can learn the new software (efficacy), recovers quickly after a bad audit (resilience) and expects next quarter to go better (optimism). Option a is right because these four are exactly the components of PsyCap. Exam trap: the three capitals sound alike. Human capital is what you know, social capital is who you know, and psychological capital is who you are and who you can become.", "traps": {"b": "Human capital (Gary Becker, 1964) is a person's knowledge, education, skills and experience, 'what you know'; it does not consist of hope or optimism.", "c": "Social capital (Pierre Bourdieu, 1986; Robert Putnam, 2000) is the value of relationships, networks and trust, 'who you know'; the four HERO resources lie inside the person.", "d": "All of these is wrong because the four resources define only psychological capital; human and social capital are separate, though they work alongside it."}}, {"q": "A person who is anxious about selection of career more appropriately needs:", "concept": "The answer is c: a person who is anxious about choosing a career most appropriately needs counselling. Counselling is a professional, face-to-face relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a basically healthy person explore a problem of living, understand himself or herself and reach his or her own decision. Guidance, by contrast, gives information, tests and advice. Career counselling grew from Frank Parsons's Choosing a Vocation (1909), whose trait-factor approach asks people to know themselves, know the world of work and match the two. Later theories added John Holland's six personality types (first proposed in 1959, later labelled RIASEC) and Donald Super's (1980) life-span, life-space theory, in which a career expresses the self-concept. Here the person has two linked needs: a decision (which field suits me?) and the anxiety around it. A counsellor can explore interests, aptitudes and values, use interest or aptitude tests where useful, help the person weigh the options, and work on the worry itself through listening, reassurance and problem solving, while leaving the final choice to the client. Option c is right because counselling addresses both the choice and the feelings, and it builds the person's own decision making rather than handing over an answer. Exam trap: 'advice' and 'suggestions' sound helpful, but they are one-way and leave the anxiety untouched; 'clinical intervention' tempts because the stem mentions anxiety, but ordinary worry about a life decision is not a mental disorder. Keep clinical intervention for diagnosable conditions with marked distress or impairment.", "traps": {"a": "Advice tells the person what to do; it is one-way, rests on the adviser's judgement, and does nothing for the anxiety or for the person's own decision making.", "b": "Suggestions are informal ideas offered by others; like advice, they skip the exploration of interests, abilities and feelings that a career decision needs.", "d": "Clinical intervention is the diagnosis and treatment of a mental disorder by a clinical psychologist or psychiatrist; normal anxiety about a career choice is a developmental problem of living, not a disorder."}}, {"q": "Which of the following principles a counselor needs to adhere to?", "concept": "The answer is d: a counsellor must adhere to beneficence, justice, fidelity and non-maleficence, so all of these apply. Ethical codes in counselling rest on a small set of moral principles. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (Principles of Biomedical Ethics, 1979) gave four: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) adapted them for counselling psychology and added fidelity, and the American Counseling Association's code now lists six, adding veracity. Beneficence means actively doing good: acting for the client's welfare. Non-maleficence means avoiding harm ('above all, do no harm'): not using techniques beyond one's competence and never exploiting the client. Justice means fairness: equal respect and access whatever the client's caste, gender, religion or income. Fidelity means faithfulness: keeping promises, honouring confidentiality and being trustworthy. Autonomy means respecting the client's right to make his or her own choices. Example: counsellor Riya keeps a student client's disclosures confidential (fidelity), refers him to a psychiatrist when she sees signs of severe depression beyond her training (non-maleficence and beneficence), and gives the same time and care to every student who comes to her (justice). Option d is right because every principle named in options a, b and c is a recognised ethical principle of counselling. Exam trap: beneficence and non-maleficence are often reversed. Beneficence is doing good; non-maleficence is refraining from harm. Fidelity is keeping trust and promises, while veracity is truthfulness; and integrity is one of the APA's general principles, not one of the four biomedical principles.", "traps": {"a": "Beneficence (doing good for the client) is a core principle, but choosing it alone leaves out justice, fidelity and non-maleficence, which apply just as much.", "b": "Justice (fairness) and fidelity (keeping trust) are both genuine principles, but this pair omits beneficence and non-maleficence.", "c": "Non-maleficence (do no harm) is a principle a counsellor must follow, yet it is only one of the listed principles, all of which are required."}}, {"q": "Non directive counselling is also known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: non-directive counselling is also known as permissive counselling. Carl Rogers (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942) proposed that the client, not the counsellor, should direct the session, because people have an inborn actualising tendency and the capacity to understand and solve their own problems. The counsellor does not advise, diagnose, interpret or steer. Instead he or she creates a warm, accepting, permissive atmosphere in which the client feels free to express any feeling without being judged, and reflects those feelings back so the client can see them clearly. He later renamed the approach client-centred (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and then person-centred, and in 1957 set out its core conditions: empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence (genuineness). Indian guidance texts call it permissive counselling because of the freedom it gives the client. Example: Kabir starts talking about exam pressure and drifts to his father's expectations; the counsellor follows, saying 'You feel you can never quite satisfy him', rather than turning him back to study tips. Option a is right because 'permissive' captures the client's freedom to lead and to express feelings, the defining mark of the non-directive method. Exam trap: the other labels belong to its rival or to nothing at all. Directive counselling (E. G. Williamson, How to Counsel Students, 1939; trait-factor, Minnesota) is counsellor-centred and is also called prescriptive; eclectic counselling (Frederick Thorne, 1950) mixes the two. Correct alternative names for the non-directive approach are client-centred, person-centred and Rogerian.", "traps": {"b": "Non-permissive counselling is the opposite of what Carl Rogers described; a controlled, counsellor-led session fits the directive approach of E. G. Williamson, not the non-directive one.", "c": "Pro-client counselling is not a recognised term; it may be confused with client-centred counselling, the real later name for Carl Rogers's approach.", "d": "Self-explanatory counselling is not a term in the counselling literature; the approach helps clients explore themselves, but it is never called this."}}, {"q": "Which of the following does not fall under the skills of counsellor?", "concept": "The answer is c: manipulation is not a skill of a counsellor. Counselling skills are learned ways of helping a client explore and resolve a problem. Allen Ivey's microcounselling (late 1960s) teaches them one at a time: attending behaviour (eye contact, posture, tone, following the client's topic), then the basic listening sequence of open and closed questions, encouragers, paraphrasing, reflection of feeling and summarising. Gerard Egan (The Skilled Helper, 1975) adds empathy and challenge. Reflection mirrors back the feeling or meaning behind the client's words ('You sound let down by your friends'). Active listening, described by Carl Rogers and Richard Farson in a 1957 paper of that name, means full attention to words, tone and body language, with responses that show understanding. Accepting the client's feelings is Carl Rogers's unconditional positive regard: valuing the person without judging what he or she feels. Manipulation is steering a person covertly, by pressure, flattery, deceit or guilt, towards ends the counsellor has chosen. It violates the client's autonomy and trust and is an ethical breach, not a skill. Example: when Arjun says he wants to leave engineering for music, his counsellor reflects his excitement and fear and helps him weigh both paths; a manipulative counsellor would quietly play on his guilt about his parents to push him back. Option c is right because it is the only option that serves the counsellor's agenda instead of the client's growth. Exam trap: do not confuse manipulation with legitimate influencing skills such as confrontation (gently pointing out discrepancies), interpretation or giving information, which are open, serve the client and form part of Allen Ivey's hierarchy.", "traps": {"a": "Reflection (of feeling or content) is a basic listening microskill in Allen Ivey's sequence and in Carl Rogers's method, so it is a genuine counsellor skill.", "b": "Active listening (Carl Rogers and Richard Farson, 1957) is the foundation of all counselling skills, not an exception to them.", "d": "Accepting the client's feelings is unconditional positive regard, one of Carl Rogers's core conditions, and a basic counselling attitude and skill."}}, {"q": "Successful people usually work on:", "concept": "The answer is c: successful, high-achieving people usually work on moderately challenging tasks. David McClelland (The Achievement Motive, 1953) measured the need for achievement (nAch), a learned desire to meet standards of excellence, by scoring the stories people tell about Thematic Apperception Test pictures. High scorers prefer moderate risks, want personal responsibility for results and seek quick feedback; in The Achieving Society (1961) he linked national levels of nAch to economic growth. John Atkinson (1957) explained the preference with his risk-taking model: the tendency to approach success equals the motive to succeed times the probability of success times the incentive value of success, and the incentive is 1 minus the probability (an easy win means little). The product of probability and incentive peaks when success is about 50 percent likely, that is, at moderate difficulty. In ring-toss studies (John Atkinson and George Litwin, 1960), people high in achievement motivation stood at middle distances from the peg, not right next to it or far away. Example: Riya, a high achiever, chooses a project her teacher calls hard but doable, rather than a trivial one or an almost impossible one. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives a real chance of success and a real sense of accomplishment. Exam trap: very easy and very hard tasks are the choices of people driven by fear of failure: an easy task makes failure unlikely, and an impossible one makes failure excusable. Do not confuse this with Edwin Locke's goal-setting theory (1968), in which specific, difficult goals raise performance.", "traps": {"a": "Very challenging tasks carry a low chance of success, so success depends on luck; in John Atkinson's model they are chosen by people high in fear of failure, for whom failing at them is excusable.", "b": "Less challenging tasks make success almost certain but give little pride or information about ability; high achievers find them unrewarding.", "d": "All of these is wrong because the achievement-motive research shows a specific preference for moderate difficulty, not for easy or extreme tasks."}}, {"q": "As per Maslow, Peak & Plateau experiences are:", "concept": "The answer is b: for Abraham Maslow, peak and plateau experiences are different. Abraham Maslow (Toward a Psychology of Being, 1962; Religions, Values and Peak-Experiences, 1964) studied self-actualising people and collected reports of peak experiences: sudden, brief, intense moments of ecstasy, wonder and awe in which the sense of self is transcended and the person feels at one with the world, as during a breathtaking sunrise, a creative breakthrough, love or music. They come unbidden and pass quickly. Late in life, notably in his preface (1970) to a new edition of Religions, Values and Peak-Experiences, he described the plateau experience: a calmer, serene and longer-lasting state of seeing the sacred or miraculous in ordinary things. It is less emotional and more cognitive than a peak, it can be cultivated and entered voluntarily, and it tends to come with maturity. Example: Kabir's peak experience is the instant he stands on a Himalayan summit at dawn, overwhelmed with awe; his plateau experience is the quiet, lasting sense of wonder he now feels watching his garden every morning. Option b is right because the two differ in intensity, duration and voluntariness, though both belong to healthy, growth-oriented living. Exam trap: 'plateau' here has nothing to do with the plateau phase of the sexual response cycle (William Masters and Virginia Johnson, 1966) or the flat stretch of a learning curve. 'Hypothetical' is a trap too: Abraham Maslow built both concepts from people's real accounts of their experiences, not from speculation.", "traps": {"a": "Same is wrong: peak experiences are sudden, intense and brief, while plateau experiences are serene, lasting and can be chosen.", "c": "Hypothetical is wrong because Abraham Maslow described both from interviews with, and written reports by, real people; they are descriptions of lived experience, not imagined constructs.", "d": "All of these cannot be right, since 'same' and 'different' contradict each other and 'hypothetical' is false."}}, {"q": "As per Herzberg's two factor theory, the hygiene factors:", "concept": "The answer is d: in Frederick Herzberg's two-factor theory, hygiene factors do none of the things listed to job satisfaction. Frederick Herzberg, with Bernard Mausner and Barbara Snyderman (The Motivation to Work, 1959), asked about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh to describe times they had felt exceptionally good or bad about their jobs (the critical incident method). The good stories centred on motivators: achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth (job content). The bad stories centred on hygiene factors: company policy and administration, supervision, salary, interpersonal relations, working conditions, status and job security (job context). His central claim is that satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one line but two separate scales. Motivators move people from no satisfaction to satisfaction. Hygiene factors move people from dissatisfaction to no dissatisfaction: when poor they cause dissatisfaction, and when good they merely remove it, without creating satisfaction. Example: when Arjun's company raises his pay and fixes the broken air conditioning, his grumbling stops, but he feels no more engaged; that comes only when his manager gives him charge of a project and recognises his work. Option d is right because hygiene factors act on dissatisfaction, not on satisfaction, so a, b and c all misdescribe them. Exam trap: hygiene factors are also called maintenance or extrinsic factors, and salary is a hygiene factor, not a motivator, even though money feels motivating. Key note: some textbooks loosely say that poor hygiene factors 'decrease satisfaction', so c has defenders, but in this model hygiene factors act on dissatisfaction, a scale separate from satisfaction; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Increasing satisfaction is what motivators (achievement, recognition, responsibility) do; improving hygiene factors only brings a person to 'no dissatisfaction'.", "b": "Saying they sometimes raise and sometimes lower satisfaction assumes a single satisfaction-dissatisfaction continuum, the traditional view Frederick Herzberg rejected.", "c": "Poor hygiene factors raise dissatisfaction, which Frederick Herzberg treats as a separate scale; they do not lower satisfaction itself, which depends on motivators."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is the most important component of an organization?", "concept": "The answer is a: people are the most important component of an organisation. An organisation is a social system: a group of people working together, in a planned structure, towards shared goals. Organisational behaviour texts such as Keith Davis and John Newstrom's Human Behavior at Work name four core elements: people, structure, technology and environment. People make up the internal social system; structure defines their formal roles and relationships; technology gives them tools and resources; and the environment (government, markets, competitors, culture) surrounds the whole. Harold Leavitt (1965) drew a similar diamond of people, task, structure and technology and argued that changing any one changes the others. People come first because the other three exist for and through them: people design the structure, choose and run the technology, and read and adapt to the environment, and organisations are formed to serve human needs. The human relations movement that grew out of the Hawthorne studies made the same point, as did Rensis Likert (The Human Organization, 1967), who argued that human assets are a firm's most valuable resource. Example: Riya's software start-up keeps its laptops, office and reporting chart, but when its senior developers resign, the work stops until new people are hired. Option a is right because without people an organisation has no purpose, no decisions and no work done. Exam trap: technology can look most important because it is the most visible or most expensive part, and structure because it appears on the organisation chart, but both are tools that people create and use. Key note: which component matters most is partly a judgement, and systems views such as Harold Leavitt's diamond stress that the elements depend on one another, so b or c could be argued; but organisational behaviour texts treat people as the central element, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Technology (machines, software, methods) is a resource that people choose, run and update; it cannot set goals or make decisions by itself.", "c": "Structure is the formal pattern of roles, levels and reporting lines; it is a framework that organises people and is empty without them.", "d": "Environment is the set of outside forces (markets, laws, competitors, culture) acting on the organisation; it surrounds the organisation rather than being its central internal component."}}, {"q": "\"When workers know people are concerned about them, their productivity increases\" demonstrates:", "concept": "The answer is b: productivity rising because workers know people are concerned about them demonstrates the Hawthorne effect. Between 1924 and 1932, studies at the Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Works near Chicago tried to find how working conditions affected output. In the illumination experiments, output rose when lighting was increased, but it also rose in the control group, and sometimes even when the light was dimmed. In the relay assembly test room, a small group of women kept producing more through many changes in rest pauses and working hours. The researchers associated with Elton Mayo (1880 to 1949), whose work Fritz Roethlisberger and William Dickson reported in Management and the Worker (1939), concluded that the workers were responding to the attention, interest and concern shown to them, and to the close-knit group they formed, more than to the physical changes. Henry Landsberger (1958) made the name Hawthorne effect famous (John R. P. French first used it in print in 1953): people change their behaviour because they know they are being observed and attended to. The studies launched the human relations movement in management, which stressed social needs, morale and informal groups. In research, the effect is a threat to validity, controlled by giving comparison groups equal attention. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly the Hawthorne finding: concern and attention, not the working conditions themselves, raised productivity. Exam trap: do not confuse it with the Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968), where others' expectations raise performance, or with demand characteristics (Martin Orne, 1962), cues that let participants guess the hypothesis. Later reanalyses found the original data weaker than the legend.", "traps": {"a": "The glass ceiling is the invisible barrier that keeps women and minorities from reaching top positions; it concerns promotion, not output rising with attention.", "c": "The framing effect (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1981) is a change in choice depending on whether the same options are worded as gains or losses; it has nothing to do with workers' output.", "d": "The anchoring effect (Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman, 1974) is the pull of an initial number on later estimates; it is a judgement bias, not a response to being cared about."}}, {"q": "An organization can usually have a:", "concept": "The answer is c: an organisation can usually have either a tall or a flat structure. Organisational structure is the formal pattern of roles, levels of authority and reporting lines. A main dimension is the span of control: the number of people who report directly to one manager. A tall structure has many levels of management and narrow spans, so each manager supervises a few people closely. It allows tight control, clear chains of command and many steps for promotion, but communication is slow and decisions travel up and down many layers. Armies, government departments and large public-sector banks are typical. A flat structure has few levels and wide spans, so each manager oversees many people. Communication is fast, employees have more autonomy and costs are lower, but managers can be overloaded and promotion paths are short. Start-ups and small firms are typical. Tom Burns and G. M. Stalker (The Management of Innovation, 1961) linked such choices to the environment: rigid, mechanistic organisations suit stable settings, while flexible, organic ones suit changing settings. Example: Kabir's 25-person design studio, where everyone reports to two partners, is flat; his mother's bank, with clerk, officer, branch manager, regional manager and head office above, is tall. Option c is right because both tall and flat structures are common, legitimate designs, so either may be found. Exam trap: students reverse the spans. Tall means narrow span, flat means wide span. And 'flat' does not mean 'without hierarchy': even a flat firm keeps some levels of authority.", "traps": {"a": "A tall structure (many levels, narrow spans) is common, but so is a flat one; choosing tall alone leaves out the other usual design.", "b": "A flat structure (few levels, wide spans) is common, especially in small firms, but tall structures are just as usual, so flat alone is incomplete.", "d": "Non-hierarchical (boss-less, fully self-managing) designs exist mainly as rare experiments; nearly every organisation keeps some hierarchy, so they are not what an organisation usually has."}}, {"q": "Which of the following types of leadership involves giving of both rewards & punishments?", "concept": "The answer is c: transactional leadership involves giving both rewards and punishments. James MacGregor Burns (Leadership, 1978) distinguished transactional leaders, who lead through an exchange with followers, from transforming leaders, who raise followers' motivation and values. Bernard Bass (Leadership and Performance Beyond Expectations, 1985) developed the idea into a fuller model. Transactional leadership has two main parts. Contingent reward: the leader sets targets and gives rewards (pay, bonus, praise, promotion) when they are met. Management by exception: the leader watches for mistakes and takes corrective action, including penalties, when standards are missed; in the active form the leader monitors and corrects early, and in the passive form steps in only after problems arise. So the follower's effort is bought with rewards and kept on track with sanctions. Transformational leadership, by contrast, works through the four Is: idealised influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation and individualised consideration. Example: Arjun, a sales manager, pays a bonus to every executive who meets the monthly target and cuts the incentive of those who miss it: a transaction. Option c is right because the reward-and-punishment exchange is the defining feature of transactional leadership. Exam trap: transactional and transformational sound alike. Transactional exchanges rewards for performance; transformational inspires people beyond self-interest. Bernard Bass argued that the best leaders use both, with transformational behaviour adding to the effects of the transactional exchange.", "traps": {"a": "Transformational leadership (James MacGregor Burns, 1978; Bernard Bass, 1985) motivates through vision, inspiration and individual attention, the four Is, not through a reward-and-punishment exchange.", "b": "Charismatic leadership (Max Weber's charismatic authority, 1922; Robert House, 1977) rests on followers' devotion to a leader seen as extraordinary, not on contingent rewards and penalties.", "d": "All of these is wrong because only transactional leadership is defined by trading rewards and punishments for performance."}}]});
